/* ==== INFLAM_RAW ==== */
/* ---------- PATHO 1 · Inflammation & Repair (100 items) ---------- */
const INFLAM_RAW=[
["L","What is the single most important cytokine driving the synthesis and deposition of connective tissue proteins during wound healing?",["Transforming growth factor-beta (TGF-β)","Interleukin-1 (IL-1)","Tumor necrosis factor (TNF)","Vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF)"],0,"TGF-β is the primary cytokine stimulating fibroblast migration, proliferation, and collagen/fibronectin synthesis while downregulating matrix metalloproteinases. IL-1 and TNF are primarily proinflammatory, while VEGF is mainly angiogenic."],
["L","Which selectin is constitutively stored within the intracellular Weibel-Palade bodies of endothelial cells and redistributed to the cell surface upon activation?",["L-selectin","E-selectin","P-selectin","CD31 (PECAM-1)"],2,"P-selectin is pre-formed and stored in the Weibel-Palade bodies of endothelial cells (and alpha granules of platelets), moving rapidly to the surface upon histamine or thrombin. E-selectin requires de novo transcription induced by cytokines (TNF, IL-1)."],
["L","What specific anatomical name is given to tissue-resident macrophages located within the liver sinusoids?",["Kupffer cells","Microglial cells","Sinus histiocytes","Alveolar macrophages"],0,"Liver = Kupffer cells. Microglial cells reside in the CNS, sinus histiocytes in the spleen and lymph nodes, and alveolar macrophages in the pulmonary alveoli."],
["L","Which morphological pattern of acute inflammation is characterized by an outpouring of cell-poor, watery fluid into body cavities lined by the peritoneum, pleura, or pericardium?",["Fibrinous inflammation","Purulent inflammation","Serous inflammation","Granulomatous inflammation"],2,"Serous inflammation presents with watery, low-protein, cell-poor fluid derived from plasma or mesothelial secretions, without bacterial infection or dense leukocytic infiltrates. Fibrinous has large fibrin deposits, purulent has pus and abundant neutrophils, granulomatous is a chronic cellular form."],
["L","Which white blood cell granule component is a highly cationic protein capable of destroying helminthic parasites but also damaging host epithelial cells?",["Major basic protein (MBP)","Myeloperoxidase","Histamine","Lysozyme"],0,"MBP is packaged inside eosinophil specific granules and binds negatively charged parasite membranes, killing helminths but causing host epithelial cytotoxicity. Myeloperoxidase is found in neutrophils, histamine in mast cell granules, lysozyme in monocytes and granulocytes."],
["L","Under standard conditions, what leukocyte population typically replaces neutrophils as the dominant cellular infiltrate in acute inflammation within 24 to 48 hours?",["Basophils", "B lymphocytes", "Natural killer cells", "Monocytes / Macrophages"],3,"Neutrophils predominate in the first 6 to 24 hours because they are more numerous and respond rapidly, but they undergo apoptosis and are replaced by longer-lived monocytes/macrophages within 24 to 48 hours."],
["L","True or False: A keloid represents a scar that grows excessively but remains strictly within the original wound boundaries and spontaneously regresses within months.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — a keloid extends beyond the margins of the original wound and rarely regresses. A hypertrophic scar stays confined to the margin and typically flattens and regresses over time."],
["L","What transformed cell type, characterized by abundant pale pink cytoplasm resembling epithelial cells, forms the core cellular hallmark of granulomas?",["Epithelioid cells (histiocytes)","Langhans giant cells","Fibroblasts","Plasma cells"],0,"Activated macrophages in granulomatous inflammation accumulate abundant cytoplasm that mimics squamous epithelial cells — hence 'epithelioid'. These can fuse into multinucleated Langhans giant cells."],
["L","Which cleavage fragments of the complement cascade serve as anaphylatoxins that directly stimulate mast cells to degranulate and release histamine?",["C1q and C2a","C3b and iC3b","C3a and C5a","C5b and C9"],2,"C3a and C5a act directly as anaphylatoxins, binding to mast cell G-protein coupled receptors to cause immediate degranulation of preformed histamine. C3b is mainly an opsonin, while C5b-9 forms the membrane attack complex."],
["L","What cell type, possessing contractile properties and smooth-muscle-like actin filaments, is responsible for wound contraction during secondary union?",["Myofibroblasts","Resting fibroblasts","Endothelial cells","Basal keratinocytes"],0,"Myofibroblasts migrate into granulation tissue, align across the wound, and contract to reduce the defect surface area by 90–95%. Normal resting fibroblasts synthesize collagen but lack robust contractile capability."],
["L","True or False: Vasodilation in acute inflammation develops primarily in large elastic arteries and is induced exclusively by leukotrienes.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — vasodilation involves mainly arterioles and postcapillary venules and is induced primarily by histamine and nitric oxide, producing the classic heat (calor) and redness (rubor)."],
["L","A localized collection of purulent exudate (pus) buried deep within a tissue, organ, or enclosed space is termed an:",["Ulcer","Abscess","Effusion","Granuloma"],1,"An abscess is a focal, walled-off collection of purulent exudate surrounded by preserved neutrophils and fibrovascular tissue. An ulcer is a surface defect, an effusion is fluid in a cavity, and a granuloma is an organized chronic aggregate of epithelioid macrophages."],
["L","At what precise microvascular anatomical site does the majority of increased vascular permeability and leukocyte emigration occur during acute inflammation?",["Postcapillary venules","Capillaries","Arterioles","Large elastic arteries"],0,"Endothelial cell contraction, histamine binding, and leukocyte diapedesis occur predominantly across postcapillary venules. Capillaries and arterioles are mainly involved in adjusting resistance and volumetric flow."],
["L","What microscopic finding is specifically pathognomonic for asbestosis, consisting of golden-brown fusiform or beaded rods with a translucent core?",["Councilman bodies", "Russell bodies", "Langhans cells", "Asbestos (ferruginous) bodies"],3,"Asbestos bodies are inorganic fibers coated with hemosiderin and ferritin by alveolar macrophages — beaded golden-brown dumbbell rods. Councilman bodies are apoptotic hepatocytes, Langhans cells are multinucleated histiocytes, Russell bodies are immunoglobulin-filled plasma cells."],
["L","On what specific cell surface are L-selectin molecules expressed?",["Leukocytes (tips of microvilli)","Endothelial cells only","Platelets only","Erythrocytes"],0,"L-selectin is leukocyte-specific (microvilli tips), E-selectin is restricted to endothelial cells, and P-selectin is found on platelets and endothelial cells."],
["L","True or False: A fully healed surgical wound recovers 100% of its original unwounded tensile strength by the end of the third month.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — a healed wound recovers only approximately 70% to 80% of its original unwounded tensile strength by 3 months. Scar tissue never re-attains 100% mechanical resilience."],
["L","Central nervous system resident macrophages that act as the primary immune scavengers of neural tissue are known as:",["Astrocytes","Ependymal cells","Microglial cells","Oligodendrocytes"],2,"Microglial cells constitute the resident mononuclear phagocyte system of the brain and spinal cord. Astrocytes are support cells, oligodendrocytes form CNS myelin, and ependymal cells line the ventricles."],
["L","What term is given to the process where a persistent fibrinous exudate in a body cavity (e.g., pericardium) is invaded by fibroblasts and capillary ingrowth, converting it into a dense fibrous scar?",["Suppuration", "Resolution", "Regeneration", "Organization"],3,"When fibrinous exudate is not fully cleared by fibrinolysis and macrophages, it is replaced by vascular and fibroblastic granulation tissue — a conversion termed organization. This obliterates serosal cavities."],
["L","What term describes an excessive accumulation of granulation tissue that protrudes above the skin margins and physically blocks re-epithelialization?",["Keloid","Exuberant granulation (proud flesh)","Contracture","Hypertrophic scar"],1,"Exuberant granulation, historically called 'proud flesh', refers to uninhibited vascular fibroblastic tissue projecting beyond the epidermis, impeding migration of epithelial cells."],
["L","Match each tissue macrophage with its correct anatomical location: 1. Liver 2. Spleen and lymph nodes 3. Lungs 4. CNS. Options: A. Microglial cells B. Kupffer cells C. Alveolar macrophages D. Sinus histiocytes.",["1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-A","1-A, 2-B, 3-D, 4-C","1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D","1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B"],0,"Liver = Kupffer cells (B); spleen/lymph nodes = sinus histiocytes (D); lungs = alveolar macrophages (C); CNS = microglia (A)."],
["L","Which pathophysiological mechanism distinguishes an inflammatory exudate from a non-inflammatory transudate?",["Exudates result entirely from increased renal hydrostatic pressure","Exudates require an increase in endothelial vascular permeability, allowing protein leakage","Transudates contain high concentrations of fibrin and cellular debris","Transudates are triggered directly by histamine-mediated endothelial gaps"],1,"Exudate formation is driven by inflammatory widening of endothelial junctions (increased microvascular permeability), allowing large proteins and leukocytes to escape. Transudates occur across intact, normally permeable endothelia from pure physical Starling imbalances."],
["L","Explain why blood flow velocity decreases (stasis) in postcapillary venules shortly after acute inflammation is initiated.",["Arteriolar vasodilation with heavy fluid/protein loss concentrates erythrocytes, markedly increasing viscosity and slowing flow","Leukotrienes actively pump blood backward into the venules","Capillary spasm closes the entire microcirculation","Increased oncotic pressure pulls fluid back into the lumen"],0,"The rapid escape of water and proteins leaves behind packed, highly concentrated red blood cells. This dramatic increase in local viscosity, paired with widened vessel lumens, produces congestion and stasis."],
["L","True or False: Selectin-mediated rolling interactions exhibit high affinity and slow dissociation rates to firmly freeze leukocytes onto the vascular endothelium.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — selectin interactions are low-affinity with fast dissociation rates, causing transient bind/detach/roll. Firm adhesion requires subsequent high-affinity integrin-ligand binding."],
["L","How does immediate transient endothelial retraction differ mechanistically from direct endothelial injury?",["Endothelial retraction is caused by bacterial toxins and causes cellular detachment", "Endothelial injury lasts only seconds and is restricted to lymphatic capillaries", "Endothelial retraction is mediated by histamine, short-lived (15–30 min), and reversible without cell death", "Endothelial injury is mediated exclusively by C3a and causes no structural damage"],2,"Endothelial retraction is a reversible physiological response to mediators like histamine and bradykinin, lasting 15–30 minutes. Endothelial injury involves cellular necrosis, detachment, and mechanical damage (e.g., burns, lytic toxins) that persists for days until repaired."],
["L","By what dual mechanisms does TGF-β achieve a net accumulation of extracellular matrix during tissue repair?",["Suppresses all fibroblast activity and promotes apoptosis", "Stimulates MMPs while inhibiting collagen synthesis", "Increases vascular permeability and recruits neutrophils", "Stimulates collagen and fibronectin synthesis by fibroblasts while inhibiting matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs)"],3,"TGF-β accelerates matrix production while blocking matrix degradation via TIMP induction and MMP downregulation. Both levers operate in tandem."],
["L","What are the four characteristic histologic zones observed in a chronic peptic ulcer, sequentially from the luminal base downward?",["Mature dense collagen, granulation tissue, active neutrophils, mucosal epithelium","Fibrinopurulent exudate, necrotic tissue/fibrinoid necrosis, granulation tissue, fibrous scar","Giant cell granulomas, caseous core, lymphocytic ring, hyperplastic muscularis","Thrombus layer, normal submucosa, myofibroblastic band, myxoid stroma"],1,"The luminal surface shows a necrotic fibrinopurulent exudate with viable neutrophils. Below is a zone of non-specific fibrinoid necrosis, supported by active vascular granulation tissue resting on a deep foundation of mature fibrous scar."],
["L","True or False: The biological activity of TGF-β in tissues is predominantly controlled by activating gene transcription rather than by post-transcriptional activation of latent precursors.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — tissue TGF-β activity is primarily regulated by post-transcriptional activation of latent TGF-β complexes and interactions with ECM molecules. Latent TGF-β is already present; activity depends on proteolytic cleavage and matrix interactions."],
["L","Why does chronic inhalation of inorganic asbestos fibers drive progressive pulmonary interstitial fibrosis rather than resolving spontaneously?",["Asbestos fibers directly activate mast cells to release histamine indefinitely","Fibers cannot be enzymatically cleared by alveolar macrophages, causing persistent inflammasome activation and chronic cytokine release","Asbestos induces severe acute serous effusions that prevent leukocyte diapedesis","Fibers transform fibroblasts into malignant Langhans multinucleated cells"],1,"Macrophages ingest asbestos fibers but cannot digest the crystalline structure, causing continuous NLRP3 inflammasome activation, chronic release of ROS, proteases, and fibrogenic growth factors (TGF-β) that drive relentless collagen deposition."],
["L","What are the three classic and fundamental clinical outcomes of an acute inflammatory response?",["Necrosis, apoptosis, and autophagy only", "Resolution, cicatrization, and calcification only", "Suppuration, ulceration, and abscess formation only", "Complete resolution; healing by connective tissue replacement (scarring); progression to chronic inflammation"],3,"Acute inflammation resolves completely if injury is mild and regeneration is possible; it leads to scarring when substantial tissue destruction occurs; and it transitions to chronic inflammation if the noxious stimulus persists."],
["L","Why do eosinophils utilize antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC) via degranulation against helminths rather than classic phagocytosis?",["Helminths lack cell surface antigens capable of activating complement", "Helminth surfaces are entirely neutral and repel phagocyte integrins", "Eosinophils lack the actin cytoskeleton required for phagocytic cup formation", "Helminths are physically far too large to be engulfed by individual leukocytes"],3,"Multicellular parasites dwarf leukocytes, making engulfment impossible. Instead, eosinophils bind parasite-coating IgE antibodies via Fc receptors and extrude granule cytotoxins (like MBP) directly onto the organism's cuticle."],
["L","True or False: In addition to stimulating scar tissue synthesis, TGF-β also serves as an anti-inflammatory cytokine that suppresses lymphocyte proliferation.",["TRUE","FALSE"],0,"TRUE — TGF-β plays a dual role: it is the master driver of fibrosis in repair, but also extinguishes inflammatory cascades by dampening lymphocyte proliferation and inhibiting activation of other leukocytes."],
["L","Which inflammatory morphology typically emerges when severe microvascular injury creates gaps large enough for massive plasma protein complexes to pass into the serosal space?",["Serous inflammation","Fibrinous inflammation","Suppurative inflammation","Ulcerative inflammation"],1,"Fibrinogen is a large macromolecule that cannot pass through minor leaks. Only marked permeability allows fibrinogen into serosal linings, where it is cleaved into pink, thread-like fibrin meshes — fibrinous inflammation."],
["L","Name the three distinct concentric microscopic zones of an established abscess from the interior outward.",["Central liquefied necrotic core (dead cells/pus), middle zone of preserved neutrophils, outer zone of vascular dilation, fibroblastic proliferation, and fibrous wall","Central zone of living neutrophils, middle fibrous wall, outer necrotic core","Inner granulation tissue, middle pus, outer preserved parenchyma","Central caseous necrosis, middle Langhans giant cells, outer serous fluid"],0,"The core contains liquefied dead cells and leukocytes, bordered by living neutrophils combating the infection, all encased in a vascular, fibroblastic healing envelope."],
["L","Why does a hypertrophic scar typically soften, flatten, and regress over several months, whereas a keloid continues to expand?",["Hypertrophic scars undergo delayed calcification and sloughing", "Keloids contain high levels of neutrophils that prevent remodeling", "Collagen degradation by MMPs eventually balances and exceeds production in hypertrophic scars as cellularity decreases", "Hypertrophic scars completely lack myofibroblasts and collagen"],2,"Hypertrophic scars undergo normal wound maturation: as myofibroblasts contract and cellularity decreases, MMPs degrade surplus collagen, softening the scar. Keloids maintain continuous abnormal collagen synthesis without remodeling equilibrium."],
["L","True or False: Acute purulent meningitis typically demonstrates an increase in lymphocytes with completely normal CSF glucose levels.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — acute purulent bacterial meningitis shows a marked preponderance of neutrophils and markedly reduced glucose. Viral meningitis shows lymphocyte predominance and normal glucose."],
["L","Provide two distinct physiological reasons why neutrophils arrive at an inflammatory site before monocytes.",["They do not require endothelial adhesion at all", "They are smaller and pass directly through arteriolar walls", "They are produced locally in the tissues while monocytes come from bone marrow", "They are far more numerous in the circulation and respond more rapidly to chemoattractants and to rapidly induced endothelial selectins"],3,"Neutrophils constitute the majority of peripheral leukocytes, have high sensitivity to early chemokines, and latch swiftly to rapidly expressed P- and E-selectins."],
["L","Which of the following is NOT one of the three core morphological hallmarks of chronic inflammation?",["Mononuclear leukocytic infiltration (macrophages, lymphocytes, plasma cells)","Extensive edema and widespread neutrophilic infiltration","Progressive tissue destruction induced by offending agents or inflammatory cells","Attempts at healing via angiogenesis and fibrosis"],1,"Edema and neutrophil predominance are hallmarks of acute inflammation. Chronic inflammation is defined by mononuclear infiltration, ongoing destruction, and simultaneous repair attempts (angiogenesis and fibrosis)."],
["L","True or False: Wound contraction during secondary union is primarily accomplished by the migratory pull of basal keratinocytes.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — basal keratinocytes perform re-epithelialization across the surface, whereas the mechanical contraction pulling wound edges together is executed by contractile myofibroblasts deep in the wound."],
["L","How does a foreign body granuloma differ immunologically from an immune granuloma?",["Foreign body granulomas require high levels of IL-2 and cytotoxic T cells","Immune granulomas are driven by a persistent T cell-mediated immune response, whereas foreign body granulomas form around inert materials without T cell immunity","Foreign body granulomas contain caseous necrosis, whereas immune granulomas never do","Immune granulomas lack epithelioid macrophages"],1,"Immune granulomas arise when an antigen induces a persistent type IV hypersensitivity (e.g., TB). Foreign body granulomas form around non-antigenic particles (sutures, talc) without an adaptive T-cell cascade."],
["L","What are the two principal bodily origins of fluid in a serous pleural or peritoneal effusion?",["Neutrophil degranulation and mast cell lysis", "Bile reflux and urinary backflow", "Lymphatic rupture and venous thrombosis", "Plasma leaking through hyperpermeable postcapillary vessels, and secretions from irritated, hyperactive mesothelial cells"],3,"Fluid can accumulate either as filtered plasma from local microvascular leakage or from the direct synthetic secretory output of irritated mesothelial cells."],
["L","A 58-year-old male with severe congestive heart failure develops bilateral pleural effusions. Fluid aspiration reveals clear, low-protein fluid with a specific gravity of 1.008 and virtually no leukocytes. What is the fundamental physical driving force responsible for this accumulation?",["Increased microvascular endothelial permeability due to histamine release","Increased pulmonary venous hydrostatic pressure forcing fluid across intact capillary walls","Extensive fibrinoid necrosis of the visceral pleura","Inflammasome activation triggering cytokine-induced vascular gaps"],1,"This fluid meets the exact criteria for a transudate (clear, low protein, cell-poor). In heart failure, venous congestion elevates downstream hydrostatic pressure, pushing fluid out through structurally intact endothelium."],
["L","A 22-year-old female sustains a superficial partial-thickness thermal scald on her forearm, developing multiple tense, clear fluid-filled epidermal blisters within two hours. What pathological pattern of inflammation does this represent?",["Fibrinous inflammation","Suppurative inflammation","Serous inflammation","Granulomatous inflammation"],2,"Burn blisters represent the classic prototype of cutaneous serous inflammation — thin, cell-poor, watery fluid pooling beneath damaged epidermis. Fibrinous forms membranes, suppurative creates pus, granulomas are chronic cellular lesions."],
["L","A 19-year-old college student presents with high fever, photophobia, severe headache, and nuchal rigidity. Lumbar puncture reveals cloudy CSF, opening pressure 280 mm water, 4,500 cells/µL (94% neutrophils), protein 220 mg/dL, glucose 18 mg/dL (serum 95). Which etiology is most consistent?",["Acute lymphocytic (viral) meningitis", "Aseptic chemical meningitis", "Tuberculous (chronic) meningitis", "Acute pyogenic (bacterial) meningitis"],3,"Cloudy/purulent CSF, profound neutrophil elevation, high protein, and marked hypoglycorrhachia represent the classic profile of acute pyogenic bacterial meningitis. Viral meningitis shows lymphocytes and normal glucose."],
["L","A patient with a severe pollen allergy experiences sudden periorbital edema, rhinorrhea, and conjunctival redness within 5 minutes of exposure. Which pharmacological mechanism would most directly counteract the primary mediator driving this immediate vascular permeability?",["A matrix metalloproteinase-9 inhibitor","An H1 receptor antagonist","A TGF-β receptor blocker","An anti-integrin alpha-4 antibody"],1,"The immediate transient phase of vascular permeability and vasodilation after IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation is driven by histamine binding to endothelial H1 receptors. H1 antagonists block this rapid pathway."],
["L","A 45-year-old male with end-stage renal disease develops uremic pericarditis with a bread-and-butter friction rub. Echocardiography shows extensive fibrinous exudate. If this exudate fails to undergo adequate fibrinolysis and clearance, what long-term sequela is expected?",["Spontaneous resolution without residual anatomical alteration","Development of an acute suppurative pyogenic abscess","Organization into fibrous scar tissue causing pericardial space obliteration and constrictive pericarditis","Widespread microglial hyperplasia throughout the epicardium"],2,"When fibrinous pericarditis fails to resolve, granulation tissue invades the fibrin mesh (organization), replacing it with dense fibrous scar that obliterates the cavity and may constrict cardiac filling."],
["L","A 28-year-old male presents with a raised, firm, erythematous scar over his sternum 6 weeks after sternotomy. It is prominent and thickened but strictly confined within the incision boundaries. What is the diagnosis and its natural course?",["Keloid; will continue to proliferate endlessly past the margins", "Cicatricial contracture; will severely limit rib cage expansion", "Exuberant granulation; must be excised immediately to avoid malignant transformation", "Hypertrophic scar; will gradually soften, flatten, and regress over several months"],3,"Confined to the original incision margin and developing rapidly after surgery, this is a hypertrophic scar, which contains myofibroblasts and typically regresses over time through matrix remodeling. Keloids cross the wound borders and do not regress."],
["L","During the migration of a neutrophil through extravascular connective tissue toward a focal source of bacteria, which cellular mechanism provides directional propulsion?",["Endothelial contraction squeezing the leukocyte forward like a pump","Cytoskeletal reorganization extending filopodia at the leading edge pulled forward by front-wheel-drive mechanics","C3a molecules mechanically dragging the leukocyte via Fc receptors","Passive Brownian motion secondary to increased local hydrostatic pressure"],1,"Leukocytes move directionally along chemoattractant gradients by reorganizing their actin-myosin cytoskeleton, projecting filopodia/pseudopods at the leading edge that pull the rear of the cell forward."],
["L","A diabetic patient with an open foot ulcer develops bright red, fleshy, highly vascular connective tissue that grows excessively above the surrounding skin edges, completely preventing inward migration of the epidermis. What is this clinical complication?",["Hypertrophic scar", "Keloid", "Exuberant granulation (proud flesh)", "Cicatricial contracture"],2,"Exuberant granulation is an overgrowth of vascularized repair tissue projecting mechanically above the dermal plane, preventing re-epithelialization until cauterized or surgically leveled."],
["L","A stool examination in an immigrant child reveals eggs of Ascaris lumbricoides, and CBC shows marked peripheral eosinophilia. What specific cytotoxic granule protein secreted by these elevated cells is responsible for penetrating the parasite's cuticle?",["Myeloperoxidase","Histaminase","Major basic protein (MBP)","Lysozyme"],2,"Eosinophils combat large helminths by extruding major basic protein, a cationic toxin that targets and destabilizes the parasitic surface tegument."],
["L","A 62-year-old retired shipyard insulator presents with progressive exertional dyspnea. Imaging shows diffuse interstitial fibrosis mainly in the lower lobes and calcified diaphragmatic pleural plaques. What is the fundamental cellular mechanism initiating this disease?",["Ingestion of persistent inorganic fibers by alveolar macrophages triggering chronic inflammasome activation", "Histamine-induced permanent arteriolar vasodilation", "Type I immediate hypersensitivity mediated by IgE against silicate crystals", "Acute neutrophil apoptosis resulting in serous pleuritis"],0,"Asbestosis begins when lower-lobe alveolar and interstitial macrophages engulf indestructible asbestos fibers, triggering the NLRP3 inflammasome → unremitting IL-1, ROS, and fibrogenic growth factors inducing diffuse fibrosis."],
["L","True or False: A mild sunburn that begins to show redness and swelling 4 hours after UV exposure and peaks the following day is an example of an immediate transient response mediated by histamine.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — sunburn represents a delayed prolonged leakage response caused by mild thermal/radiation endothelial injury, developing after 2–12 hours and lasting days. The immediate transient response is histamine-mediated and subsides within 15–30 minutes."],
["L","A 34-year-old HIV-positive patient develops chronic headache and low-grade fevers. CSF: slightly turbid, opening pressure 320 mm water, 350 leukocytes/µL (85% lymphocytes), markedly elevated protein, reduced glucose, and a delicate fibrin spiderweb coagulum on standing. Most likely diagnosis?",["Acute pyogenic (bacterial) meningitis","Viral (aseptic) meningitis","Chronic tuberculous meningitis","Cryptococcal serous transudation"],2,"Chronic tuberculous meningitis yields elevated opening pressure, lymphocytic pleocytosis (100–1,000/µL), high protein, decreased glucose, and a characteristic fibrin coagulum/pellicle. Viral meningitis retains normal glucose and does not clot."],
["L","An experimental animal is genetically engineered with a complete knockout of the genes encoding E-selectin and P-selectin. Following bacterial inoculation into subcutaneous tissue, what leukocyte function will be primarily and immediately impaired?",["Intracellular lysosomal phagocytosis","High-affinity firm adhesion via ICAM-1","Initial leukocyte rolling along the vascular endothelium","Myofibroblast-mediated contraction"],2,"Selectins (E- and P-selectin on endothelium, L-selectin on leukocytes) mediate the low-affinity capture and initial rolling phase. Without them, rolling is severely impeded, preventing downstream integrin activation and adhesion."],
["L","Following a third-degree thermal burn over the anterior elbow, a patient heals by secondary intention but develops a rigid joint deformity restricting forearm extension to only 45 degrees. What pathologic repair complication has occurred?",["Hypertrophic scar", "Keloid", "Cicatricial contracture (wound contracture)", "Exuberant granulation"],2,"Contractures represent an exaggeration of wound contraction, typically across flexion creases or after large burns, causing substantial structural distortion and joint immobility."],
["L","A patient with acute neutropenia develops an aggressive pneumonia caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa. If a lung biopsy is examined at day 4 of infection, which leukocyte is still expected to predominate, defying the rule that monocytes replace early responders within 48 hours?",["Eosinophils","Neutrophils","Plasma cells","Basophils"],1,"While monocytes normally replace neutrophils within 24–48 hours, Pseudomonas is a notable exception where continuous chemoattraction drives persistent, vigorous neutrophil recruitment for several days."],
["L","Within 90 minutes of an acute bacterial skin infection, which cytokine pair produced by tissue macrophages acts on nearby postcapillary endothelial cells to induce de novo expression of E-selectin?",["IL-10 and TGF-β","TNF and IL-1","Histamine and bradykinin","Interferon-gamma and IL-4"],1,"Macrophages encountering pathogen ligands secrete TNF and IL-1, which act on adjacent venular endothelium to induce transcription and surface expression of E-selectin (and L-selectin ligands) within 1–2 hours."],
["L","A young woman undergoes ear piercing and 4 months later presents with a large, bulbous, pedunculated 3 cm collagenous mass across the earlobe, far exceeding the initial needle puncture site. What is this lesion?",["Keloid","Hypertrophic scar","Exuberant granulation","Dermatofibroma"],0,"A scar extending beyond the original wound boundary without spontaneous regression is a keloid, common in earlobes and predisposed individuals."],
["L","True or False: In a patient with advanced cirrhosis and severe hypoalbuminemia, ascites fluid is characterized by a high protein concentration and abundant neutrophilic debris.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — cirrhosis-associated ascites from hypoalbuminemia is a transudate: low protein, low specific gravity, minimal cellular debris. Hypoalbuminemia decreases colloid osmotic pressure, unlike inflammatory exudates which are protein-rich and cell-dense."],
["L","If an experimental drug selectively upregulates matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) while blocking TGF-β receptors during the fibroplasia phase of repair, what histological outcome would be observed in the healing wound?",["Massive keloid development","Extreme exuberant granulation protruding above the epidermis","Severely deficient collagen deposition resulting in wound dehiscence","Accelerated contracture closing the wound in 48 hours"],2,"TGF-β drives collagen synthesis while suppressing MMPs. Blocking TGF-β and increasing MMP activity leads to unchecked ECM degradation and deficient collagen accumulation, compromising wound integrity and causing dehiscence."],
["L","Match each clinical scenario to its correct morphological inflammatory pattern: 1. Skin blister from a minor burn 2. Thick yellow exudate with necrotic liquefaction in acute appendicitis 3. Fibrin-coated bread-and-butter pericarditis in uremia 4. Chronic pulmonary nodule with caseous necrosis and Langhans cells. Options: A. Fibrinous B. Granulomatous C. Serous D. Purulent.",["1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B","1-A, 2-C, 3-D, 4-B","1-D, 2-B, 3-A, 4-C","1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D"],0,"Burn blisters are serous (C); appendiceal pus is purulent (D); uremic pericarditis is fibrinous (A); TB-like caseating nodules are granulomatous (B)."],["H","How do the kinetic and structural profiles of histamine-mediated endothelial leakage compare with severe burn-induced endothelial leakage?",["Histamine causes irreversible detachment lasting days; burns cause transient gaps lasting 15 minutes", "Both mechanisms require gene transcription and peak at 48 hours", "Both mechanisms affect only capillaries and are mediated by eotaxin", "Histamine causes immediate, transient venular gaps (15–30 min) without cell death; burns cause direct endothelial necrosis and detachment across all microvessels lasting hours to days"],3,"Histamine induces rapid, reversible contraction of postcapillary venular endothelial cells lasting 15–30 minutes. Direct thermal injury destroys endothelial cells by necrosis across venules, capillaries, and arterioles, creating persistent leaks enduring until thrombosis or neoangiogenesis."],
["H","Contrast the CSF laboratory profile of acute pyogenic bacterial meningitis with that of acute viral meningitis with respect to predominant cell type and glucose concentration.",["Bacterial: marked neutrophilic pleocytosis (10–10,000 cells/µL) and markedly reduced glucose (<50 mg/dL); Viral: lymphocytic pleocytosis (10–100 cells/µL) and normal glucose","Bacterial: lymphocytic pleocytosis and normal glucose; Viral: neutrophilic pleocytosis and reduced glucose","Bacterial: eosinophilic pleocytosis and elevated glucose; Viral: no cells and no glucose change","Both show identical neutrophilic pleocytosis and low glucose"],0,"Bacteria elicit rapid neutrophil recruitment and consume glucose via metabolic activity, while viruses elicit an adaptive lymphocytic response without significant CSF glucose consumption."],
["H","When analyzing a chronic inflammatory infiltrate, which distinguishing feature confirms the diagnosis of granulomatous inflammation over non-specific chronic inflammation?",["Presence of diffuse interstitial plasma cells and lymphocytes", "Marked predominance of apoptotic neutrophils within venular lumens", "Abundant red blood cell extravasation and capillary thrombosis", "Focal, organized aggregation of transformed epithelioid macrophages surrounded by a collar of lymphocytes"],3,"Non-specific chronic inflammation shows loosely distributed mononuclear cells. Granulomatous inflammation requires a distinct architecture: a circumscribed nodule of activated, epithelioid-transformed macrophages, often with Langhans giant cells and a lymphocytic collar."],
["H","True or False: Both hypertrophic scars and keloids contain excessive collagen, but hypertrophic scars have more myofibroblasts and typically regress, whereas keloids extend past the original margins and persist.",["TRUE","FALSE"],0,"TRUE — hypertrophic scars are rich in myofibroblasts, stay confined to wound margins, and regress as MMPs balance synthesis. Keloids produce exuberant, thick collagen bundles that invade adjacent normal tissue and rarely regress."],
["H","How does the anatomical and structural distribution of pulmonary fibrosis in asbestosis differ from that in classic silicosis and coal workers' pneumoconiosis?",["Asbestosis begins in the lower lobes and subpleurally, whereas silicosis predominantly targets the upper lobes","Asbestosis affects only the trachea, while silicosis is restricted to parietal pleura","Silicosis begins in the subpleural lower lung bases and spares lymph nodes","Asbestosis forms non-collagenous granulomas, whereas silicosis forms purely serous cysts"],0,"Asbestosis has a predilection for the lower lobes and subpleural zones, progressing upward. In contrast, silicosis and coal workers' pneumoconiosis typically show nodular lesions primarily involving the upper lung zones."],
["H","Analyze the difference in Starling forces governing transudate formation in right-sided congestive heart failure versus kwashiorkor/nephrotic syndrome.",["Heart failure elevates microvascular hydrostatic pressure, driving fluid outflow; kwashiorkor and nephrotic syndrome reduce plasma albumin, lowering intravascular colloid osmotic pressure","Heart failure reduces colloid osmotic pressure; kwashiorkor increases hydrostatic pressure","Both are driven by increased vascular permeability from histamine","Heart failure is due to lymphatic obstruction; kwashiorkor is due to venous congestion"],0,"In heart failure, venous congestion increases upstream hydrostatic pressure. In severe malnutrition or renal protein loss, depleted plasma proteins diminish the inward-pulling oncotic force, allowing fluid to leak into the interstitium."],
["H","In examining a time-course curve of acute inflammation, which factor best explains why neutrophil counts drop precipitously in tissues after 24 to 48 hours?",["Neutrophils undergo rapid apoptosis within extravascular tissues due to their short lifespan","Endothelial cells stop expressing all integrins and selectins within 12 hours","Macrophages directly phagocytose viable, functional neutrophils to prevent tissue injury","TGF-β stimulates neutrophils to differentiate into fibroblasts"],0,"Neutrophils are short-lived effector cells programmed to undergo apoptosis within 24–48 hours after extravasation. Concurrently, longer-lived monocytes arrive and become the predominant population."],
["H","A patient with severe, uncontrolled chronic bronchial asthma shows extensive epithelial desquamation and mucosal denudation on bronchial biopsy; washings contain high concentrations of major basic protein (MBP). What is the paradoxical role of eosinophil degranulation in this pathology?",["MBP promotes epithelial re-growth while killing opportunistic bacteria","Cationic proteins evolved to kill helminths lack selectivity and induce collateral damage to host respiratory epithelial membranes","Eosinophils secrete MBP to convert into Langhans giant cells","MBP prevents mast cell degranulation, inducing tissue necrosis"],1,"MBP is a strongly cationic toxin optimized to disrupt negatively charged parasite membranes. In allergic asthma, its non-targeted extracellular release directly lyses host respiratory epithelial cells, causing mucosal damage and airway hyperreactivity."],
["H","True or False: A wound healing by primary union undergoes extensive wound contraction mediated by myofibroblasts, resulting in a 90% reduction of original surface area.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — marked wound contraction reducing surface area by 90–95% is a feature of secondary union. Primary union involves closely approximated clean surgical edges requiring minimal contraction or granulation tissue."],
["H","Which set of conditions makes healing by complete resolution impossible, forcing the tissue toward connective tissue replacement (scarring)?",["Mild superficial abrasion with preserved basal lamina","Substantial tissue destruction involving non-dividing (permanent) cells and heavy, uncleared fibrinous exudates","Transient histamine-mediated venular leakage in skin","Acute viral infection cleared completely within 4 days"],1,"Complete resolution requires an intact extracellular framework, regenerative parenchymal cells, and swift clearance of exudates. If stroma is destroyed, cells cannot divide, or fibrin cannot be cleared, the defect must heal via organization and scarring."],
["H","Compare the mechanisms regulating endothelial expression of P-selectin versus E-selectin during the initial hours of acute inflammation.",["Both require 24 hours of transcriptional delay before expression", "P-selectin requires de novo transcription; E-selectin is pre-formed in Weibel-Palade bodies", "Both are pre-formed and stored in the same cytoplasmic granules", "P-selectin is pre-synthesized and rapidly mobilized from Weibel-Palade bodies via exocytosis (minutes) stimulated by histamine or thrombin; E-selectin requires de novo gene transcription and protein synthesis (1–2 hours) stimulated by TNF and IL-1"],3,"P-selectin redistribution occurs almost instantly as storage granules fuse with the plasma membrane, whereas E-selectin expression depends on cytokine signaling cascades and transcription."],
["H","In analyzing CSF, which feature specifically differentiates chronic tuberculous meningitis from acute lymphocytic (viral) meningitis?",["Tuberculous meningitis has marked neutrophilic pleocytosis; viral meningitis has none","Tuberculous meningitis typically displays reduced CSF glucose and may form a fibrin coagulum on standing, whereas viral meningitis has normal glucose and does not clot","Viral meningitis causes protein >500 mg/dL, while TB meningitis causes no protein increase","Viral meningitis is marked by numerous ferruginous bodies"],1,"Both present with lymphocytic pleocytosis, but tuberculous meningitis features elevated protein, consumed glucose (<50 mg/dL), and a characteristic fibrin coagulum. Viral meningitis characteristically retains normal glucose."],
["H","True or False: Pleural plaques are the most common manifestation of asbestos exposure, and their size and number correlate directly with the risk of developing mesothelioma.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — pleural plaques are the most common manifestation, but their size and number do not correlate with the level or duration of exposure. They are benign markers of prior exposure, not direct predictors of neoplastic risk."],
["H","Analysis of a 3-month-old surgical scar demonstrates tensile strength approximately 75% of normal unwounded skin. What molecular processes are responsible for this gain in strength from week 1 to month 3?",["Continued migration of large numbers of circulating monocytes","Replacement of type I collagen with type III collagen and loss of elastin","Collagen cross-linking, increased bundle diameter, and matrix remodeling by MMPs accompanied by decreased cellularity","Persistent histamine release by expanding mast cell populations"],2,"Over several months, granulation tissue becomes less vascular and less cellular, while type III collagen is replaced by sturdier type I collagen, cross-linked into larger fibrils to maximize tensile strength."],
["H","Match each description with its corresponding clinical/pathologic condition: 1. Excessive granulation tissue that prevents re-epithelialization 2. Dense collagenous growth extending beyond the boundaries of the original cut 3. Raised scar confined to original boundaries that flattens over months 4. Excessive contraction resulting in mechanical deformity across a joint. Options: A. Keloid B. Exuberant granulation C. Cicatricial contracture D. Hypertrophic scar.",["1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C","1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D","1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B","1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"],0,"Exuberant granulation blocks epithelial growth; keloids extend beyond incision borders; hypertrophic scars regress and stay within borders; contractures deform surrounding structures."],
["H","A pharmaceutical company designs a drug to prevent organ fibrosis in chronic hepatitis. Which therapeutic target would be most effective, based on the central mediator driving tissue fibrosis?",["Neutralizing antibody against TGF-β","Recombinant E-selectin protein infusion","Synthetic bradykinin receptor agonist","Leukotriene B4 inhibitor"],0,"TGF-β is the master cytokine promoting fibroblast activation, collagen deposition, and fibrosis in chronic inflammation of the liver, lungs, and kidneys. Blocking TGF-β directly interferes with this fibrogenic pathway."],
["H","A clinician evaluates an LP report: cloudy, opening pressure 220 mm H2O, 3,200 cells/µL (90% PMNs), protein 180 mg/dL, glucose 15 mg/dL (serum 100 mg/dL). The resident suggests starting antiviral therapy alone. Is this decision justified?",["Yes, because high opening pressures and cloudy appearance are standard findings in enterovirus","No — the high neutrophil count and markedly depressed CSF-to-serum glucose ratio indicate acute bacterial meningitis requiring prompt antibiotics","Yes, because protein elevation above 100 mg/dL rules out bacterial infection","No — the patient clearly has a transudate from heart failure"],1,"A markedly reduced CSF-to-serum glucose ratio (<0.4) with predominant neutrophilic pleocytosis and purulent fluid indicates bacterial, not viral, meningitis, mandating immediate empiric antibiotics."],
["H","True or False: Because TGF-β is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that inhibits leukocyte proliferation, administering high-dose systemic TGF-β would be a safe and ideal therapy to reduce scarring in post-surgical patients.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — systemic TGF-β would accelerate and worsen pathologic scarring and fibrosis because it is the primary driver of fibroblast collagen synthesis. It downregulates leukocytes but directly stimulates fibroblast migration, proliferation, and collagen deposition while inhibiting matrix breakdown."],
["H","When assessing tissue edema in acute inflammation, what clinical and therapeutic benefit is gained by distinguishing endothelial retraction from endothelial injury?",["Endothelial retraction indicates malignant transformation requiring resection", "Endothelial injury produces a transudate that can be cleared in minutes", "Retraction is mediator-driven and potentially responsive to receptor antagonists, whereas injury reflects cellular necrosis requiring structural repair", "Retraction affects only deep arteries and is refractory to pharmacological intervention"],2,"Retraction is driven by chemical mediators (e.g., histamine) binding receptors — blunted by antagonists (e.g., antihistamines). Injury reflects necrosis and detachment that cannot be reversed by receptor antagonists and resolves only with healing over days."],
["H","A 24-year-old female presents with an unsightly raised scar 5 months after ear-piercing, now three times the diameter of the original hole, invading adjacent lobular tissue. A surgeon recommends simple primary excision without adjunctive therapy. How should this recommendation be evaluated?",["Appropriate — simple excision provides a 100% cure rate for keloids","Inadequate — keloids have a very high recurrence rate following simple surgical excision alone","Inappropriate — keloids spontaneously regress if left alone another month","Appropriate — keloids are malignant neoplasms requiring wide margins"],1,"Keloids extend past original boundaries and notoriously recur, often growing back larger, if treated solely with re-excision without adjuvant treatment (e.g., intralesional corticosteroids)."],
["H","Evaluate why a large, organized fibrinous pericardial exudate poses a more serious functional threat to the heart than a simple serous pericardial effusion of the same volume.",["Both are equally dangerous and produce identical adhesions", "Serous effusions always transform into fibrous bands that constrict the heart", "A serous effusion can be readily resorbed without permanent structural alteration, whereas an organized fibrinous exudate stimulates capillary and fibroblast ingrowth, forming dense collagenous adhesions that obliterate the pericardial space and lead to constrictive pericarditis", "Fibrinous exudates are safer because they are self-limited by lymphatics"],2,"Serous fluid is cell-poor and low in fibrin, allowing lymphatics to drain it smoothly. Fibrinous exudates stimulate fibroblastic organization into rigid fibrous bands that constrict diastolic cardiac filling."],
["H","A factory worker exposed to asbestos for 25 years has routine chest radiography showing bilateral calcified diaphragmatic pleural plaques. His spirometry and physical exam are completely normal. Which clinical conclusion is most justified?",["Plaques indicate immediate conversion to malignant pleural mesothelioma", "Plaques indicate advanced honeycombing throughout the lung parenchyma", "Plaques confirm past exposure to asbestos but do not directly correlate with the severity of pulmonary impairment or exposure level", "The patient must have active bacterial suppurative pleuritis"],2,"Pleural plaques are the most common radiographic finding in asbestos exposure — circumscribed, dense collagenous scars. They are typically asymptomatic, and their size/number do not correlate with functional impairment or exposure level."],
["H","True or False: Systemic intravenous antibiotics easily sterilize and resolve a mature abscess without requiring surgical drainage, because the outer zone consists of hypervascular granulation tissue.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — antibiotics penetrate poorly into the central avascular, liquefied necrotic core of an abscess. Definitive management typically requires surgical incision and drainage."],
["H","A patient with an acute localized bacterial soft-tissue infection exhibits 95% neutrophils in the wound at 12 hours. A medical student asserts this is abnormal because macrophages should have arrived first. How should this assertion be evaluated?",["Correct — neutrophils appear only after 48 hours when chronic inflammation begins", "Correct — monocytes are the first cells to leave postcapillary venules in bacterial infections", "Incorrect — eosinophils are the primary responders to all bacterial pathogens", "Incorrect — neutrophils normally dominate the first 6 to 24 hours because they are more abundant and attach more readily to induced selectins"],3,"Neutrophils predominate in the early 6–24 hour window due to high circulating numbers, rapid chemotactic response, and high avidity for acutely induced selectins. Monocytes replace them later, between 24 and 48 hours."],
["H","A patient has persistent ascites. Fluid analysis reveals a specific gravity of 1.025, protein of 4.2 g/dL, and marked cellular debris. The internist plans to treat exclusively with dietary salt restriction for simple congestive transudation. Critique this management plan.",["Flawed — the high protein, high specific gravity, and cellular debris confirm an inflammatory exudate, mandating a workup for underlying infection, peritoneal carcinomatosis, or inflammatory disease rather than simple diuresis","Appropriate — low specific gravity always indicates uncomplicated transudate","Appropriate — cellular debris confirms simple mechanical backflow","Flawed — salt restriction is contraindicated in all ascites"],0,"Transudates typically have a low specific gravity (<1.012) and low protein (<3 g/dL). High protein and cellular debris indicate an exudate from increased vascular permeability, requiring an etiology search rather than treatment for volume overload."],
["H","Which clinical scenario has the highest likelihood of achieving complete resolution rather than healing by fibrous scarring?",["Extensive third-degree burn destroying the epidermis, dermis, and subcutaneous tissue", "Chronic duodenal ulcer penetrating through the muscularis propria", "Large myocardial infarction affecting 40% of the left ventricular free wall", "Lobar pneumonia where bacterial infection is rapidly cleared, leaving the alveolar basement membrane architecture intact"],3,"Complete resolution requires clearance of the agent without destruction of the structural matrix, in tissues with regenerative capacity (like alveolar epithelium). Myocardium cannot regenerate, and full-thickness burns or deep ulcers disrupt the framework, requiring scarring."],
["H","True or False: Removing surgical sutures at postoperative day 10 is safe because the wound has already recovered 80% of its unwounded tensile strength.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — a wound achieves only about 10% of its unwounded tensile strength at the end of the first week; 70% to 80% strength is reached only after approximately 3 months of remodeling."],
["H","In evaluating a patient with severe parasitic infection receiving anthelmintic therapy, which clinical finding would reflect collateral host tissue toxicity caused directly by eosinophil major basic protein (MBP)?",["Acute denudation and erosion of intestinal or bronchial epithelial borders","Widespread calcification of diaphragmatic pleura","Immediate liquefactive necrosis with purulent abscess formation","Obliteration of the pericardial space by fibrous organization"],0,"MBP is non-selective: while it disrupts helminthic membranes, its extracellular release damages host epithelial cells, producing mucosal denudation, shedding, and local ulceration."],
["H","A surgeon notes during follow-up that an open leg wound has stopped contracting. Histological biopsy reveals an absence of alpha-smooth muscle actin-positive cells within the granulation tissue. What cellular deficiency explains the failure of wound contraction?",["Failure of endothelial cells to sprout capillary buds","Absence of differentiated myofibroblasts within the wound matrix","Lack of mature neutrophils undergoing apoptosis","Inability of alveolar macrophages to produce major basic protein"],1,"Wound contraction is driven by myofibroblasts, which express alpha-smooth muscle actin and physically pull wound margins together. Without functional myofibroblasts, the wound cannot contract effectively."],
["H","A pathologist reviews a lung biopsy showing dense interstitial fibrosis, distorted airspaces with honeycombing, and beaded golden-brown rods with translucent cores. What is the most definitive clinical judgment regarding the etiology?",["The patient has silicosis contracted from working in a quartz quarry", "The patient has an acute serous viral pneumonitis that will resolve spontaneously", "The patient has asbestosis resulting from chronic inhalation and retention of asbestos fibers", "The findings are characteristic of a non-specific foreign body reaction to surgical sutures"],2,"Interstitial honeycombing fibrosis combined with classic asbestos (ferruginous) bodies — golden-brown beaded rods with iron-protein coats — confirms asbestosis."],
["H","A tissue-engineering laboratory aims to design an artificial matrix scaffold that accelerates wound healing while preventing hypertrophic scarring. Which combination of biochemical modifications would best achieve this therapeutic objective?",["High-dose continuous TGF-β release with complete blockade of matrix metalloproteinases", "Total inhibition of endothelial cell migration and capillary bud formation", "Continuous infusion of histamine to maintain sustained vascular permeability", "Baseline matrix deposition stimulus paired with timed, controlled MMP activation to balance collagen turnover and prevent excessive accumulation"],3,"Hypertrophic scars arise from an imbalance where collagen synthesis outpaces degradation. Controlled matrix deposition with regulated MMP activity allows proper collagen remodeling and cross-linking without excessive accumulation."],
["H","Design a rapid diagnostic triage protocol for an emergency department evaluating suspected meningitis. What sequence of tests on CSF distinguishes acute pyogenic bacterial meningitis from viral meningitis within 30 minutes?",["CSF culture and 48-hour viral PCR analysis only", "Evaluation for asbestos bodies and Weibel-Palade body exocytosis", "Measurement of TGF-β levels and major basic protein concentration", "Immediate Gram stain, differential cell count (neutrophil vs. lymphocyte %), and CSF-to-blood glucose ratio"],3,"An immediate differential showing elevated PMNs, a low CSF-to-serum glucose ratio, and visible bacteria on Gram stain confirms bacterial meningitis within minutes, whereas viral meningitis shows lymphocytic predominance and normal glucose."],
["H","Propose a targeted monoclonal antibody strategy to selectively block the very earliest step of leukocyte recruitment in an acute inflammatory flare without interfering with firm integrin adhesion.",["Develop an antibody targeting selectins (E-selectin or P-selectin) or their sialylated oligosaccharide ligands (e.g., Sialyl-Lewis X)","Develop an antibody against ICAM-1 to block integrin firm adhesion","Administer an antibody that degrades Weibel-Palade bodies","Use an antibody that enhances histamine release"],0,"Selectins mediate the initial rolling phase of recruitment. Blocking E- or P-selectin prevents rolling without directly inhibiting downstream integrin-mediated firm arrest or transendothelial migration."],
["H","To construct an in vitro model of an immune granuloma, which cellular and cytokine components must be co-cultured?",["Red blood cells, platelets, and preformed histamine", "Fibroblasts and pure transudative fluid", "Neutrophils, C3a anaphylatoxin, and endothelial Weibel-Palade bodies", "Macrophages, CD4+ T lymphocytes, and persistent antigens capable of stimulating a cell-mediated response"],3,"Immune granulomas require persistent antigens that stimulate CD4+ T helper cells to produce cytokines (like IFN-γ), which activate macrophages into epithelioid cells and multinucleated giant cells."],
["H","A burn reconstruction team is formulating a surgical protocol to minimize disabling contractures across major joints in deep second- and third-degree burns. Which mechanistic strategy should be prioritized?",["Encouraging unchecked myofibroblast activity to rapidly close skin defects", "Injecting high concentrations of major basic protein directly into the wound bed", "Early wound excision, prompt skin grafting, and active physical splinting to oppose the inward vector of myofibroblast contraction", "Suppressing matrix metalloproteinases to ensure rigid scar formation"],2,"Contractures develop from uncontrolled myofibroblast contraction across flexible joints during secondary healing. Early grafting reduces secondary contraction, while splinting counters myofibroblastic forces during matrix remodeling."],
["H","Formulate a minimal set of three pathogenic criteria necessary to experimentally induce an acute abscess in animal soft tissue.",["Implantation of inert suture, diffuse edema, and mast cell degranulation", "Injection of sterile saline, serous exudation, and lymphocytic cuffing", "Viral inoculation, epithelioid cell transformation, and caseous necrosis", "Inoculation of pyogenic bacteria (e.g., Staphylococcus aureus), induction of localized liquefactive tissue necrosis, and massive recruitment and entrapment of neutrophils within the necrotic focus"],3,"Abscesses require pyogenic organisms that trigger localized liquefactive necrosis and draw a dense influx of neutrophils that liquefy into pus, walled off by fibrovascular repair tissue."],
["H","Based on the four histologic layers of a chronic peptic ulcer, how does a mucosal protectant (such as sucralfate, which binds positively charged proteins in necrotic tissue) facilitate healing?",["It converts the deep scar layer into epithelial mucosa","It coats the superficial fibrinopurulent necrotic layer, shielding the underlying granulation tissue from acid-peptic digestion to allow re-epithelialization","It induces apoptosis of all fibroblasts and inhibits angiogenesis","It permanently paralyzes muscularis propria contractions"],1,"By coating the surface fibrinopurulent necrotic slough, the protectant prevents gastric acid from degrading newly formed granulation tissue and allows mucosal epithelial cells to migrate across the bed."],
["H","True or False: In advanced pulmonary asbestosis with widespread honeycombing, treatment with recombinant MMPs can reverse the architectural damage and restore normal alveoli.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"FALSE — advanced honeycombing involves permanent architectural destruction of alveolar parenchyma. Degrading collagen cannot regenerate lost basement membranes or alveoli and can cause tissue collapse."],
["H","A clinician evaluates an exuberant granulation ('proud flesh') lesion on a patient's healing surgical wound. Construct an appropriate management plan that will allow the wound to successfully complete healing.",["Administer systemic antibiotics and allow the tissue to grow unimpeded", "Apply a tight tourniquet proximal to the limb to induce ischemic necrosis", "Inject TGF-β into the lesion to stimulate collagen formation", "Perform chemical cauterization (e.g., silver nitrate) or surgical excision to bring the tissue level down to the surrounding skin plane, enabling re-epithelialization"],3,"Exuberant granulation physically protrudes above the skin, creating a mechanical barrier to advancing epidermal edges. Reducing it to the dermal level with cautery or excision allows keratinocytes to migrate across and close the wound."],
["H","A diagnostic panel is being designed to monitor the progression of acute inflammation into chronic inflammation in deep-seated post-surgical sites. Which set of biological markers would indicate a transition to chronic inflammation?",["Disappearance of neutrophils accompanied by rising titers of TGF-β, persistent monocyte-derived macrophages, and fibroblastic markers","A sudden spike in histamine accompanied by pure serous transudation","Total absence of lymphocytes and preservation of original capillary architecture","Complete resolution of edema without collagen deposition"],0,"The acute-to-chronic transition is marked by clearance of short-lived neutrophils, persistence of monocyte-derived macrophages, sustained cytokine signaling (TGF-β), and replacement of acute exudates with fibroblastic proliferation and collagen deposition."],
];;

/* ==== IMMUNE_RAW ==== */
/* ---------- PATHO 2 · Diseases of the Immune System (100 items) ---------- */
const IMMUNE_RAW=[["L","Inheritance of the HLA-B27 allele confers a 100- to 200-fold increased relative risk for which of the following inflammatory conditions?",["Rheumatoid arthritis","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Multiple sclerosis","Ankylosing spondylitis"],3,"Individuals carrying the HLA class I allele HLA-B27 exhibit a 100- to 200-fold increased risk of developing ankylosing spondylitis. Rheumatoid arthritis is associated with HLA-DRB1 alleles, while multiple sclerosis and SLE are associated with other HLA class II alleles (such as DRB11501 and DRB10301)."],["L","Which cytokine secreted by Th2 helper cells is specifically responsible for recruiting and activating eosinophils during a Type I hypersensitivity reaction?",["Interleukin-13 (IL-13)","Interleukin-10 (IL-10)","Interleukin-5 (IL-5)","Interleukin-4 (IL-4)"],2,"IL-5 is the cardinal Th2 cytokine that stimulates the growth, differentiation, recruitment, and activation of eosinophils. In contrast, IL-4 and IL-13 drive B-cell immunoglobulin class switching to IgE."],["L","Natural killer (NK) cells recognize the Fc tail of IgG antibodies bound to target cells to mediate antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC) via which surface receptor?",["CD3","CD4","CD16","CD28"],2,"CD16 is the Fcγ receptor expressed on NK cells that binds the Fc portion of IgG, triggering target cell lysis via ADCC. CD3 is a T-cell marker, CD4 is a helper T-cell coreceptor, and CD28 provides costimulation for T-cell activation."],["L","Name the signature cytokine secreted by Th1 cells that functions as the most potent activator of macrophages in classical macrophage activation.",["Interleukin-4 (IL-4)", "Interleukin-5 (IL-5)", "Interferon-gamma (IFN-γ)", "Interleukin-13 (IL-13)"],2,"IFN-γ is released by Th1 lymphocytes and natural killer cells to induce classical (M1) macrophage activation. This pathway upregulates microbicidal substances, such as reactive oxygen species and lysosomal enzymes, to eradicate intracellular pathogens."],["L","Match each CREST syndrome component (1-5) with its clinical definition (A-E). Components: 1. Calcinosis  2. Raynaud phenomenon  3. Esophageal dysmotility  4. Sclerodactyly  5. Telangiectasia. Definitions: A. Thickening/tightening of skin on fingers and hands  B. Dilation of superficial capillaries producing red marks  C. Subcutaneous calcium deposition  D. Paroxysmal cold/emotion-provoked digital vasospasm  E. Distal esophageal smooth-muscle atrophy with reflux",["1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D, 5-E","1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-E, 5-A","1-D, 2-E, 3-A, 4-B, 5-C","1-C, 2-D, 3-E, 4-A, 5-B"],3,"CREST syndrome comprises Calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, Esophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, and Telangiectasia. It represents the limited cutaneous variant of systemic sclerosis."],["L","Identify the two ribonucleoprotein-directed autoantibodies that serve as key serologic markers for diagnosing Sjögren syndrome.",["Anti-Scl-70 and anti-centromere","Anti-histone and anti-dsDNA","SS-A (anti-Ro) and SS-B (anti-La)","Anti-Sm and anti-RNP"],2,"SS-A (Ro) and SS-B (La) are autoantibodies against ribonucleoproteins characteristically found in patients with Sjögren syndrome. They are also linked to subacute cutaneous lupus and neonatal lupus erythematosus with congenital heart block."],["L","Which autoantibody is characteristically present in more than 95% of patients presenting with drug-induced lupus erythematosus?",["Anti-dsDNA", "Anti-Smith (Sm)", "Anti-histone", "Anti-centromere"],2,"Drug-induced lupus erythematosus (provoked by hydralazine, procainamide, isoniazid, or D-penicillamine) is characterized by antibodies directed against histones. Anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm are specific for idiopathic SLE, whereas anti-centromere antibodies characterize limited systemic sclerosis."],["L","Which HIV envelope glycoprotein mediates the final transmembrane fusion of the viral envelope with the host inflammatory cell membrane?",["gp120","gp41","p24","p17 matrix protein"],1,"While gp120 mediates initial attachment to host CD4 and chemokine coreceptors (CCR5/CXCR4), the resulting conformational change exposes the hydrophobic fusion peptide of gp41. gp41 then inserts directly into the host membrane to drive membrane fusion."],["L","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome is caused by a mutation in the gene encoding WASP located on which chromosome?",["Chromosome Xp11.23", "Chromosome 6p", "Chromosome 22q11.2", "Chromosome 7q31"],0,"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome is an X-linked recessive disorder mapped specifically to locus Xp11.23, which encodes the Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome Protein (WASP). WASP couples membrane receptors to the actin cytoskeleton."],["L","Inherited deficiencies of which terminal complement components predispose individuals specifically to recurrent, invasive infections caused by Neisseria species?",["C5, C6, C7, C8, and C9 (membrane attack complex)","C1 inhibitor (C1-INH)","C1q, C2, and C4 (early classical pathway)","C3 and factor B (amplification pathway)"],0,"The terminal complement proteins C5b–C9 assemble the Membrane Attack Complex (MAC), which punches lytic pores in microbial membranes. Because Neisseria meningitidis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae have exceptionally thin cell walls, MAC-mediated lysis is essential for their clearance."],["L","Contact dermatitis induced by poison ivy (urushiol) is classified under which Gell and Coombs hypersensitivity category?",["Type I (Immediate)","Type II (Antibody-mediated)","Type III (Immune complex-mediated)","Type IV (T cell-mediated / Delayed)"],3,"Contact dermatitis is a prototypical delayed-type (Type IV) hypersensitivity reaction initiated by hapten-protein conjugates activating CD4+ and CD8+ T lymphocytes. Tissue injury occurs via cytokines and direct cytotoxicity rather than antibodies."],["L","True or False: In patients with intact immune systems and zero known risk factors for tuberculosis, an induration of 10 mm on a tuberculin skin test (PPD) is classified as positive.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"Correction: For individuals with no known risk factors for tuberculosis, an induration must measure 15 mm or greater to be classified as positive. A 10 mm cutoff applies to individuals with specific clinical or occupational risk factors."],["L","Which of the following antinuclear antibodies is considered the most specific for systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), despite being present in only 20% to 30% of patients?",["Anti-Smith (Sm) antibody", "Anti-histone antibody", "Anti-Ro/SS-A antibody", "Anti-centromere antibody"],0,"Anti-Smith (anti-Sm) antibodies are directed against the core proteins of small nuclear ribonucleoprotein particles and are virtually diagnostic/pathognomonic for SLE. Generic ANAs are sensitive but not specific, whereas anti-histone is linked to drug-induced lupus."],["L","What characteristic microscopic finding on histopathology identifies Cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection in colonic endothelial and mesenchymal cells?",["Owl's eye intranuclear inclusions", "Caseating granulomas with Langhans giant cells", "Negri bodies", "Koilocytic atypia with binucleation"],0,"CMV-infected cells exhibit prominent cytomegaly with large basophilic \"owl's eye\" intranuclear inclusions surrounded by a clear halo, accompanied by smaller basophilic/eosinophilic cytoplasmic granules."],["L","Chronic Granulomatous Disease (CGD) most commonly results from an X-linked inherited mutation affecting which catalytic subunit of phagocyte NADPH oxidase?",["p47phox", "gp91phox", "p67phox", "Myeloperoxidase"],1,"The most frequent variant of CGD is an X-linked recessive defect in gp91phox, a membrane-bound component of the cytochrome b558 complex in phagocyte NADPH oxidase. Autosomal recessive forms involve cytoplasmic subunits p47phox and p67phox."],["L","In myasthenia gravis, autoantibodies bind directly to which postsynaptic transmembrane receptor protein?",["Muscle-specific kinase (MuSK)", "Voltage-gated calcium channel (P/Q type)", "Acetylcholine receptor (nicotinic AChR)", "Presynaptic voltage-gated potassium channel"],2,"Myasthenia gravis is driven by IgG autoantibodies targeting nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the motor endplate. This prevents acetylcholine binding and accelerates receptor internalization/degradation, yielding progressive muscle weakness."],["L","On renal biopsy light microscopy of diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis (Class IV), circumferential thickening of glomerular capillary loops resembling rigid wire loops is primarily caused by which process?",["Deposition of subepithelial amyloid fibrils","Extensive subendothelial immune complex deposition","Proliferation of podocyte foot processes","Direct detachment of the glomerular basement membrane"],1,"The characteristic \"wire loop\" lesions in Class IV lupus nephritis represent massive subendothelial immune complex deposition causing marked thickening of the glomerular capillary wall."],["L","Patients diagnosed with Sjögren syndrome have an increased lifetime risk of developing which specific lymphoid malignancy?",["Follicular lymphoma","Classic Hodgkin lymphoma","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","Extranodal marginal zone B-cell lymphoma (MALToma)"],3,"Sustained polyclonal B-cell hyperactivation and lymphocytic infiltration of glandular tissues in Sjögren syndrome predispose patients to monoclonal expansion and the emergence of extranodal marginal zone B-cell lymphomas."],["L","Which autoantibody is strongly correlated with diffuse systemic sclerosis and portends an increased risk of rapid skin progression and early visceral involvement?",["Anti-centromere antibody", "Anti-U1 RNP", "Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP)", "Anti-DNA topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70)"],3,"Anti-DNA topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70) is specific for diffuse systemic sclerosis and marks patients at high risk for widespread cutaneous fibrosis and early pulmonary/renal complications. Anti-centromere antibodies are conversely associated with limited scleroderma (CREST)."],["L","Name the retroviral enzyme packaged inside the HIV nucleocapsid that converts single-stranded viral genomic RNA into double-stranded complementary DNA.",["Reverse transcriptase","Integrase","Protease","Host RNA polymerase II"],0,"Reverse transcriptase carries out RNA-dependent DNA polymerization followed by DNA-dependent DNA synthesis to produce proviral double-stranded cDNA. Integrase subsequently incorporates this DNA into the host genome.--- Understanding (21-40) ---"],["L","Why does inheriting an HLA allele such as HLA-B27 or HLA-DRB1 markedly increase an individual's susceptibility to autoimmune disease without acting as a direct cause?",["The HLA molecule directly mutates self-antigens into foreign epitopes", "The allele causes immediate systemic deletion of peripheral CD4+ T regulatory cells", "The HLA molecule preferentially binds and displays specific self-peptides to autoreactive T cells", "The allele stimulates constitutive degranulation of tissue-resident mast cells"],2,"Specific HLA class I and class II cleft architectures exhibit variable binding affinities, preferentially presenting select self-derived or modified peptides to T cells. However, because environmental triggers and failures of peripheral tolerance are also required, most individuals carrying these alleles never develop clinical disease."],["L","True or False: Natural killer cells spare healthy host tissues because their activating surface receptors are continuously suppressed by self-MHC class I molecules engaging inhibitory receptors.",["TRUE","FALSE"],0,"Healthy nucleated host cells constitutively express MHC class I molecules, which engage inhibitory NK receptors (such as KIRs) to suppress cytotoxic signaling. Loss of surface MHC class I during viral infection or oncogenesis releases this inhibition, allowing activating signals to trigger target cell lysis."],["L","During an allergic reaction, which molecular event directly causes the degranulation of sensitized mast cells upon secondary allergen exposure?",["Allergen binding directly to toll-like receptor 4", "Secretion of high titers of IgM into the extracellular space", "Activation of the terminal complement cascade with C9 pore assembly", "Cross-linking of allergen-specific IgE bound to high-affinity FcεRI receptors"],3,"Secondary allergen exposure cross-links adjacent IgE molecules pre-bound to FcεRI receptors on the mast cell membrane. This triggers intracellular signaling cascades, rapid exocytosis of preformed granules (histamine, proteases), and de novo lipid mediator synthesis."],["L","In systemic sclerosis, activated CD4+ T cells infiltrate injured perivascular tissues and secrete which two primary profibrotic cytokines that stimulate fibroblasts to overproduce collagen and extracellular matrix?",["TNF-α and IL-1","TGF-β and IL-13","IL-4 and IL-5","IFN-γ and IL-2"],1,"Repeated microvascular endothelial injury recruits T cells that secrete profibrotic mediators, predominantly TGF-β and IL-13. These cytokines stimulate quiescent fibroblasts to differentiate into myofibroblasts, leading to excessive deposition of collagen and progressive organ fibrosis."],["L","In Sjögren syndrome, what is the primary pathophysiologic mechanism leading to keratoconjunctivitis sicca and xerostomia?",["Immune complex-mediated vasculitis obliterating the facial arteries","T- and B-lymphocytic infiltration and fibrosis of the lacrimal and salivary glands","Autoantibody-mediated overstimulation of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors","Complement deficiency preventing clear fluid secretion from acinar structures"],1,"Sjögren syndrome is characterized by dense periductal lymphocytic infiltrates (primarily CD4+ helper T cells, B cells, and plasma cells) in lacrimal and salivary glands. The progressive immune-mediated destruction of acini and secondary fibrosis eliminates tear and saliva secretion."],["L","Contrast the cellular tropism of early-stage versus late-stage HIV strains by naming the chemokine coreceptor targeted by M-tropic strains and T-tropic strains, respectively.",["Both strains use only CCR5","Both strains use only CXCR4","M-tropic strains use CCR5; T-tropic strains use CXCR4","M-tropic strains use CXCR4; T-tropic strains use CCR5"],2,"Early in infection, sexually transmitted and dominant viral strains are M-tropic (R5), utilizing the CCR5 coreceptor found on macrophages and memory CD4+ T cells. Later in the disease, viral mutations frequently yield T-tropic (X4) variants that utilize CXCR4, infecting naive T cells and thymic precursors, accelerating CD4+ T-cell depletion."],["L","Why does HIV replicate efficiently in activated CD4+ T cells but fail to produce productive infection in resting, naive T cells?",["Resting T cells degrade viral capsids via hyperactive proteasomes", "Resting cells lack cell-surface CD4 receptors entirely", "T-cell activation induces NF-κB, which binds the HIV LTR promoter to drive proviral transcription", "Activation causes spontaneous loss of host cell genomic integrity"],2,"Cytokine stimulation and antigen encounter trigger nuclear translocation of host NF-κB in T cells. HIV has evolved promoter binding sequences in its long terminal repeat (LTR) that hijack host NF-κB to drive high-level viral transcription. Furthermore, naive T cells contain active APOBEC3G, which restricts replication."],["L","True or False: Patients with inherited deficiencies in early classical pathway components (C1q, C2, C4) typically present with more severe and lethal pyogenic infections than patients lacking the C3 component.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"Correction: C3 deficiency is much more severe than early classical deficiencies because C3 represents the mandatory convergence point for all three complement pathways (classical, alternative, and lectin). Deficiencies in C1, C2, or C4 leave the alternative and lectin pathways intact, producing milder infections and lupus-like immune clearance defects."],["L","In the pathogenesis of rheumatoid arthritis, what is the nature of the \"pannus\" that destroys the articular cartilage and subchondral bone?",["An acellular collection of necrotic bone fragments surrounded by neutrophils", "An invasive squamous metaplasia arising from the articular joint lining", "A dense calcified deposit within the synovial fluid mimicking chondrocalcinosis", "A mass of hyperplastic synovial tissue rich in inflammatory cells, granulation tissue, and fibroblasts"],3,"The inflamed synovium in rheumatoid arthritis undergoes marked papillary hyperplasia, hypervascularization, and infiltration by CD4+ T cells, macrophages, and plasma cells, forming a \"pannus\". This pannus bridges across joint margins, releasing proteases and activating osteoclasts that destroy cartilage and subchondral bone."],["L","Explain why patients with Chronic Granulomatous Disease develop widespread granuloma formation in affected organs despite their inability to generate a respiratory burst.",["Compensatory recruitment and collection of activated macrophages attempting to wall off microbes when the defective neutrophil response fails to clear the infection", "Antibody-mediated opsonization with renal immune complex deposition", "Direct viral transformation of macrophages into epithelioid cells", "Excessive neutrophil degranulation causing acute liquefactive necrosis"],0,"Because phagocyte NADPH oxidase is nonfunctional, neutrophils cannot synthesize superoxide anions ($O_2^{\\bullet-}$) and hypochlorite ($HOCl$) to eliminate ingested pathogens. In response, chronic cell-mediated inflammatory signaling recruits waves of activated macrophages that physically wall off the surviving organisms, producing characteristic granulomas."],["L","What fundamental pathophysiologic distinction separates a Type II hypersensitivity reaction from a Type III hypersensitivity reaction?",["Type II is cell-mediated by CTLs, whereas Type III is mediated exclusively by IgE","Type II involves antibodies binding to fixed cellular or tissue antigens, whereas Type III involves circulating antigen-antibody immune complexes","Type II requires prior mast cell sensitization, whereas Type III occurs immediately upon first antigen encounter","Type II reactions never activate complement, whereas Type III reactions rely solely on IgA"],1,"In Type II hypersensitivity, antibodies (IgG or IgM) bind to fixed antigens on specific cells, basement membranes, or extracellular matrix, causing localized injury. In Type III, soluble antigens and antibodies form circulating immune complexes that deposit randomly into vascular walls and glomeruli, inciting diffuse neutrophilic inflammation."],["L","True or False: In patients with Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, immunologic evaluation reveals markedly elevated serum levels of IgM and severe, uncompensated depletion of serum IgG.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"Correction: Patients with Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome typically exhibit characteristically LOW serum IgM, normal levels of IgG, and ELEVATED levels of IgA and IgE."],["L","Why does the induration of a positive tuberculin skin test (PPD) reach its peak intensity at 24 to 72 hours rather than immediately upon intradermal injection?",["Preformed histamine in mast cells requires 48 hours to diffuse through the dermis", "Complement proteins require up to 3 days to assemble the membrane attack complex in dermic capillaries", "Memory CD4+ Th1 cells must traffic to the injection site, recognize antigens presented by dendritic cells, and secrete cytokines to recruit macrophages", "Antibodies to tuberculin protein are synthesized de novo by naive B cells over 72 hours"],2,"Type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity requires time for antigen-presenting cells to process the intradermal tuberculin antigens, present them to circulating memory Th1 cells, and release cytokines (e.g., IFN-γ) that slowly recruit and activate mononuclear phagocytes, producing a delayed cellular infiltrate peaking at 24–72 hours."],["L","State the primary mechanism of cell clearance and tissue destruction responsible for anemia in autoimmune hemolytic anemia.",["Direct CD8+ T-cell cytotoxicity against erythrocyte precursors","Reduced renal erythropoietin production","Opsonization of erythrocytes by IgG/IgM and complement with phagocytosis in the spleen and liver","Complement-mediated intravascular lysis only"],2,"Autoantibodies bind to surface Rh or I antigens on erythrocytes. The Fc portions and fixed C3b fragments are recognized by Fc and complement receptors on macrophages in the reticuloendothelial system, causing extravascular phagocytosis and cell lysis."],["L","Deficiencies of the C1 inhibitor (C1-INH) protein result in hereditary angioedema through which of the following mechanisms?",["Direct overstimulation of mast cell IgE receptors by C1q","Unregulated activation of kallikrein and the contact system, leading to excessive generation of bradykinin","Excessive deposition of terminal membrane attack complex in mucosal venules","Severe depletion of factor H leading to uncontrolled alternative pathway lysis"],1,"C1-INH is a critical serpin that inhibits both the initial classical complement protease complexes (C1r/C1s) and kallikrein of the kinin cascade. Its deficiency causes unregulated kallikrein activation, generating excessive levels of the potent vasoactive peptide bradykinin, producing episodes of nonpitting angioedema."],["L","True or False: Patients with systemic lupus erythematosus who test positive for antiphospholipid antibodies may display a false-positive serologic test for syphilis.",["TRUE","FALSE"],0,"Syphilis screening tests (VDRL/RPR) utilize cardiolipin as the testing antigen. Because SLE patients with antiphospholipid syndrome frequently harbor anticardiolipin antibodies, they react positively in these assays despite having negative confirmatory tests (such as FTA-ABS)."],["L","How does the functional outcome of autoantibody binding in Graves disease differ from that in myasthenia gravis?",["Graves antibodies stimulate receptor activity, whereas myasthenia gravis antibodies inhibit receptor activity","Graves antibodies destroy thyroid follicles via ADCC, whereas myasthenia gravis causes muscle hypertrophy","Graves antibodies form circulating immune complexes, whereas myasthenia gravis is purely a T cell-mediated insulitis","Graves disease is mediated by IgM, whereas myasthenia gravis is mediated entirely by IgE"],0,"In Graves disease, autoantibodies bind the TSH receptor on thyroid follicular cells and mimic TSH, driving unregulated thyroid hormone synthesis (hyperthyroidism). In myasthenia gravis, autoantibodies bind acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction, directly blocking transmission and accelerating receptor internalization (functional inhibition)."],["L","Explain how repeated microvascular injury contributes to the characteristic cutaneous and visceral fibrosis seen in systemic sclerosis.",["Endothelial injury releases neutrophil elastase, producing immediate scar formation within hours","Endothelial injury causes loss of terminal complement, leading to recurrent Neisseria infection","Endothelial injury causes perivascular inflammation and release of TGF-β/IL-13, triggering progressive fibroblast collagen deposition","Endothelial injury causes IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation and isolated acute edema"],2,"Chronic, repeated microvascular endothelial insults induce luminal narrowing, ischemic damage, and platelet activation. Infiltrating perivascular T cells and macrophages continuously release TGF-β and other profibrotic cytokines, stimulating persistent extracellular matrix synthesis and pervasive tissue sclerosis."],["L","A pediatric patient born with a severe congenital immunodeficiency disorder is characterized clinically by which predominant presentation?",["Unregulated neoplastic proliferation of mature memory B lymphocytes","Impaired innate and/or adaptive immune responses causing recurrent, opportunistic, and multisystem infections","Selective resistance to encapsulated pyogenic bacteria but intolerance to dietary gluten","Exaggerated histamine release causing spontaneous, continuous anaphylactoid shocks"],1,"Congenital (primary) immunodeficiencies stem from genetic mutations disrupting innate (complement, phagocytes) or adaptive (B and T lymphocytes) defenses. They manifest with severe, recurrent, persistent, and opportunistic infections across the respiratory, gastrointestinal, and cutaneous systems."],["L","True or False: Drug-induced lupus is strongly associated with the inheritance of the HLA-DR4 allele.",["TRUE","FALSE"],0,"Individuals carrying the HLA-DR4 allele (particularly slow acetylators) are at significantly higher risk of developing drug-induced lupus when exposed to offending medications like hydralazine or procainamide.--- Applying (41-60) ---"],["L","A 19-year-old female presents with acute sneezing, clear rhinorrhea, and nasal mucosal edema minutes after stroking a domestic cat. Applying your knowledge of Type I hypersensitivity, what initial molecular cytokine cascade induced her naive CD4+ helper T cells to differentiate into allergen-specific Th2 cells?",["Direct cross-linking of IgE on naive CD4+ T cells","Complement C3a/C5a anaphylatoxin-mediated Th2 differentiation","IL-12 and IFN-γ secretion by dendritic cells","Secretion of the epithelial alarmins IL-25, IL-33, and TSLP, which drive Th2 polarization"],3,"When mucosal epithelial cells and antigen-presenting cells encounter aeroallergens, they release IL-25, IL-33, and thymic stromal lymphopoietin (TSLP). These epithelial-derived cytokines stimulate naive CD4+ T cells to differentiate into Th2 effector cells."],["L","A neoplastic tumor cell downregulates its cell-surface HLA Class I molecules to escape cytotoxic CD8+ T-lymphocyte surveillance. What will be the biological outcome when an NK cell encounters this tumor cell?",["The NK cell will undergo immediate apoptotic programmed cell death","The NK cell will become anergic due to lack of co-stimulation","The NK cell will lyse the tumor cell because inhibitory killer cell signals are lost","The NK cell will hyper-express CD4 receptors to recruit memory B lymphocytes"],2,"NK cells employ a \"missing self\" recognition system. Loss of surface MHC class I relieves the inhibitory signals that normally restrain NK cells; activating receptors triggered by stress ligands on the tumor cell now dominate, leading to direct cytotoxicity and target cell lysis."],["L","A 52-year-old woman complains that her fingers turn pale white and then blue and painful when handling frozen foods. Examination reveals tight, thickened, shiny skin over her fingers, dilated red marks on her face, and subcutaneous nodules on her elbows. If serologic testing is performed, which autoantibody is most likely to be elevated?",["Anti-centromere antibody","Anti-DNA topoisomerase I (Scl-70)","Anti-Smith antibody","Anti-histone antibody"],0,"The patient displays Raynaud phenomenon, sclerodactyly, telangiectasia, and calcinosis—cardinal features of limited systemic sclerosis (CREST syndrome). CREST syndrome is classically linked to anti-centromere antibodies, whereas anti-Scl-70 denotes diffuse scleroderma."],["L","A 56-year-old female presents with persistent dry eyes, severe dry mouth, and bilateral enlargement of her parotid glands. A biopsy of a minor labial salivary gland is obtained. What histopathologic finding is expected?",["Extravascular caseating granulomas with Langhans giant cells","Periductal and periacinar lymphocytic infiltrates (CD4+ T cells, B cells, plasma cells) with fibrosis","Severe neutrophilic vasculitis with fibrinoid necrosis","Extensive deposition of Congo red-positive amyloid in striated ducts"],1,"Sjögren syndrome produces extensive chronic lymphocytic infiltrates (predominantly CD4+ T helper cells, B lymphocytes, and plasma cells) surrounding ducts and acini of exocrine glands, ultimately causing glandular atrophy and replacement fibrosis."],["L","A 62-year-old man receiving long-term procainamide for cardiac arrhythmia presents with arthralgias, malar erythema, pleuritic chest pain, and a positive ANA. Serum anti-histone antibodies are markedly positive, while anti-dsDNA is negative. Applying clinical pharmacopathology, what is the single most definitive intervention to resolve his clinical syndrome?",["Start high-dose cyclophosphamide","Initiate plasmapheresis","Add anti-TNF biologic therapy","Discontinue the offending drug (procainamide)"],3,"Drug-induced lupus is typically a self-limiting autoimmune condition that remits following the withdrawal of the causative agent (e.g., procainamide, hydralazine, isoniazid). Severe renal and CNS involvement is exceedingly rare in this variant compared to idiopathic SLE."],["L","A pharmacology team designs a small molecule that blocks the chemokine coreceptor CCR5 on the surface of human macrophages and memory T cells. This drug would be most therapeutically effective during which clinical phase of HIV infection?",["Acute infection and early asymptomatic clinical latency dominated by M-tropic (R5) viruses","Terminal AIDS phase when CXCR4-tropic strains predominate","After proviral integration into the host genome has completed","During viral budding when p24 capsid proteins assemble"],0,"Early in HIV infection, transmission and dominant viral replication rely on CCR5-tropic (M-tropic, R5) strains. Targeting CCR5 blocks the conformational steps of gp120/gp41 required for viral fusion. It is ineffective against CXCR4-tropic strains that predominate in late-stage disease."],["L","An 8-month-old boy presents with recurring episodes of epistaxis, scattered petechiae over his trunk, intractable pruritic flexural eczema, and recurrent otitis media. A complete blood count reveals marked thrombocytopenia with abnormally small platelets. Which defect explains his clinical presentation?",["Defective phagocyte NADPH oxidase superoxide generation", "Absent developmental migration of neural crest cells to the 3rd and 4th pharyngeal pouches", "Inherited deficiency in the WASP protein affecting cytoskeletal actin assembly in immune cells and platelets", "Congenital loss of the terminal complement membrane attack complex C5–C9"],2,"The triad of Thrombocytopenia (with small platelets), Immunodeficiency (recurrent infections), and Eczema (\"TIE\") defines Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome. It is caused by mutations in the WASP gene at Xp11.23, which is critical for linking cell surface receptor signaling to the actin cytoskeleton."],["L","An 18-year-old college student presents with acute fever, nuchal rigidity, purpura fulminans, and petechial skin lesions. Cerebrospinal fluid cultures confirm Neisseria meningitidis. A history reveals two prior hospitalizations for Neisseria bacteremia. Applying immunology principles, which defect is most likely present?",["Deficiency in complement components C5, C6, C7, C8, or C9", "Mutation in gp91phox of phagocyte oxidase", "Mutation in the WASP gene", "Selective absence of serum IgA"],0,"Recurrent systemic infections with encapsulated Neisseria species (N. meningitidis or N. gonorrhoeae) are the hallmark of inherited deficiencies in the terminal Membrane Attack Complex (MAC) components (C5–C9)."],["L","A 38-year-old male with poorly controlled HIV infection (CD4+ count: 180 cells/µL) undergoes a routine screening tuberculin skin test (PPD). At 48 hours, the examining physician measures 6 mm of palpable induration across the forearm. How should this test result be clinically interpreted?",["The tuberculin test is POSITIVE (≥5 mm cutoff applies to immunocompromised/HIV patients)", "The result is indeterminate and must be repeated in 2 weeks", "The test is positive only if induration reaches ≥15 mm", "The tuberculin test is NEGATIVE because induration is under 10 mm"],0,"In severely immunocompromised individuals, including patients living with HIV, an induration threshold of 5 mm or greater is clinically classified as positive. This lower cutoff accounts for their diminished capacity to mount a robust cell-mediated delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction."],["L","A 28-year-old male with poorly controlled Type 1 diabetes has pancreatic islet biopsy demonstrating dense infiltrates of CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes and CD4+ Th1 cells surrounding destroyed beta cells. What is the primary mechanism of beta-cell death in this condition?",["Classical pathway complement lysis induced by anti-insulin IgM antibodies","Perforin/granzyme-mediated apoptosis by CTLs accompanied by inflammatory cytokines from Th1/Th17 cells","Granulomatous foreign-body reaction to excess glycogen granules","Immediate histamine and leukotriene degranulation by islet mast cells"],1,"Type 1 diabetes is a Type IV cell-mediated autoimmune disease. Pancreatic beta-islet cells are selectively targeted and destroyed by antigen-specific CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes (via perforin/granzyme and Fas/FasL) and local inflammatory cytokines (IFN-γ, TNF) produced by CD4+ Th1/Th17 cells."],["L","According to the classification criteria for Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE), what are two major peripheral blood cytopenias that fulfill hematologic disease criteria?",["Hemolytic anemia, leukopenia/lymphopenia, or thrombocytopenia", "Pancytopenia exclusively from marrow fibrosis", "Isolated neutrophilia with reactive eosinophilia", "Polycythemia with reactive thrombocytosis"],0,"SLE generates autoantibodies targeting peripheral blood cells, manifesting as autoimmune hemolytic anemia, leukopenia/lymphopenia, or immune thrombocytopenia."],["L","A 34-year-old female presents with bilateral ptosis, diplopia, and generalized muscle fatigue that progressively worsens toward the evening but improves following rest. Serum testing reveals antibodies blocking nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. Which pathophysiologic mechanism accounts for her symptoms?",["Depletion of synaptic vesicle acetylcholine stores in the presynaptic neuron","Inhibition of neuromuscular transmission due to antibody blockade and down-regulation of postsynaptic ACh receptors","Extensive fibrinoid necrosis of the motor endplate microvasculature","CD8+ T-cell mediated demyelination of peripheral motor axons"],1,"In myasthenia gravis, autoantibodies bind nicotinic acetylcholine receptors on the postsynaptic muscle membrane, competitively inhibiting acetylcholine binding, increasing receptor endocytosis, and causing fluctuating skeletal muscle weakness."],["L","A 42-year-old HIV-positive male with a CD4+ count of 35 cells/µL presents with progressive vision loss, painful swallowing (odynophagia), and profuse watery diarrhea. A colonoscopy reveals extensive mucosal ulcerations, and biopsy shows markedly enlarged endothelial cells with prominent basophilic intranuclear inclusions surrounded by halos. What pathogen is causing this presentation?",["Cryptosporidium parvum","Candida albicans","Mycobacterium avium complex","Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"],3,"CMV causes severe end-organ disease in advanced AIDS (CD4+ &lt; 50 cells/µL), typically presenting as chorioretinitis, esophagitis, and colitis. The pathognomonic histology shows cytomegaly with \"owl's eye\" intranuclear inclusions."],["L","A 24-year-old female with SLE presents with worsening facial edema, hypertension, microscopic hematuria, and 3.5 g/24 hr proteinuria. A renal biopsy demonstrates Class IV diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis. What molecular finding directly explains the subendothelial \"wire loop\" appearance of the capillary loops?",["In situ anti-GBM antibody linear deposition along type IV collagen","Massive subendothelial accumulation of circulating antigen-antibody immune complexes","Intracellular precipitation of viral proteins inside podocytes","Transudation of lipid vesicles secondary to podocyte foot process effacement"],1,"Class IV diffuse lupus nephritis is an immune complex-mediated glomerulonephritis. Massive subendothelial deposition of DNA-anti-dsDNA immune complexes leads to marked, circumferential thickening of the capillary basement membranes, creating the \"wire loop\" pattern seen on light microscopy."],["L","True or False: In a child with Chronic Granulomatous Disease, catalase-negative bacterial infections (Streptococci) are cleared normally because the bacteria produce endogenous hydrogen peroxide that the patient's phagocytes can utilize for bacterial killing.",["TRUE","FALSE"],0,"Catalase-negative bacteria generate metabolic $H_2O_2$ but do not produce catalase to degrade it. In CGD, myeloperoxidase utilizes this microbial hydrogen peroxide to produce hypochlorite, successfully killing the pathogen. Catalase-positive organisms (S. aureus, Aspergillus) destroy their own $H_2O_2$, leaving the CGD phagocyte defenseless."],["L","A 48-year-old woman with active rheumatoid arthritis refractory to methotrexate is prescribed an agent targeting TNF-α. Applying your knowledge of RA immunopathology, neutralizing TNF-α will directly decrease which of the following processes?",["Formation of terminal C5b-9 complement complexes on red blood cells","Synovial inflammation, macrophage activation, and osteoclast-mediated bone erosion","B-cell apoptosis in germinal centers","Immediate mast cell degranulation in the joint space"],1,"TNF-α, secreted by Th1/Th17 cells and activated synovial macrophages, plays a pivotal role in RA by inducing synovial cell proliferation, collagenase expression, and RANKL-mediated osteoclast activation, driving joint destruction."],["L","Name the clinical term used to describe dry eyes caused by lacrimal gland destruction, and the term used to describe dry mouth resulting from salivary gland destruction in Sjögren syndrome.",["Uveitis and glossitis","Blepharitis and sialadenitis","Chemosis and halitosis","Keratoconjunctivitis sicca (dry eyes) and xerostomia (dry mouth)"],3,"Sjögren syndrome specifically targets the exocrine glands of the head and neck. Destruction of the lacrimal apparatus yields keratoconjunctivitis sicca, while destruction of the major and minor salivary glands causes xerostomia."],["L","A medical technician tests blood for early acute HIV infection. Which viral protein is the major capsid antigen universally measured in diagnostic screening assays to detect early antigenemia prior to seroconversion?",["gp120", "gp41", "p17", "p24"],3,"The p24 protein is the major capsid protein of HIV-1. Because it is synthesized in large quantities during early viral replication, fourth-generation HIV ELISA assays test for free p24 antigen alongside anti-HIV antibodies to narrow the diagnostic window."],["L","A 22-year-old male presents to the emergency department with acute swelling of his lips, tongue, and larynx causing stridor. He notes no antecedent hives, wheezing, or pruritus, and denies food or drug allergies. His father and paternal uncle experienced similar episodes. Laboratory testing reveals low levels of functional C1-INH and low C4. Applying biochemical pathophysiology, what mediator directly caused his localized vascular permeability and edema?",["Platelet-activating factor","Bradykinin","Histamine","Leukotriene C4"],1,"In hereditary angioedema (C1-INH deficiency), unregulated kallikrein cleaves high-molecular-weight kininogen into bradykinin. Bradykinin is a potent mediator of vascular permeability, producing episodic mucosal and submucosal edema without the mast-cell-driven urticaria seen in anaphylaxis."],["L","True or False: Goodpasture syndrome is a Type II hypersensitivity disease mediated by autoantibodies targeting noncollagenous domains of type IV collagen in the basement membranes of kidney glomeruli and pulmonary alveoli.",["TRUE","FALSE"],0,"In Goodpasture syndrome, autoantibodies bind directly to the noncollagenous domain of the α3 chain of type IV collagen in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes. This triggers complement fixation and Fc receptor-mediated leukocyte recruitment, causing rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis and pulmonary hemorrhage.=== SECTION 2: HOTS QUESTIONS (61-100) ===--- Analyzing (61-75) ---"],["H","Which set of clinical, serologic, and prognostic features correctly distinguishes Diffuse Systemic Sclerosis from Limited Systemic Sclerosis (CREST)?",["Diffuse has anti-centromere antibodies, prominent calcinosis, and benign renal prognosis; Limited has anti-Scl-70 antibodies and early renal crisis", "Diffuse is mediated exclusively by IgE mast cell degranulation; Limited is mediated by CD8+ T-cell perforin pores", "Diffuse lacks vascular changes entirely; Limited shows marked destructive necrotizing vasculitis of the central nervous system", "Diffuse exhibits widespread rapid skin thickening, anti-DNA topoisomerase I (Scl-70) antibodies, and early visceral involvement; Limited exhibits skin involvement restricted to the distal extremities/face, anti-centromere antibodies, and late visceral involvement"],3,"Diffuse systemic sclerosis features early, rapid, and widespread skin involvement, an aggressive course with early visceral (renal/pulmonary) damage, and anti-Scl-70 seropositivity. Limited scleroderma features fibrosis confined to the hands, forearms, and face, protracted late visceral complications (such as pulmonary arterial hypertension), and anti-centromere antibodies."],["H","Match each disease with its primary immunopathologic mechanism: 1. Autoimmune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)  2. SLE glomerulonephritis  3. Poison ivy contact dermatitis  4. Atopic bronchial asthma. Mechanisms: A. Type I (IgE cross-linking with mast cell degranulation)  B. Type II (antibody-mediated opsonization/clearance)  C. Type III (circulating immune complex deposition)  D. Type IV (T-cell mediated delayed hypersensitivity)",["1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B","1-D, 2-A, 3-B, 4-C","1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A","1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D"],2,"ITP is a classic Type II cytotoxic process (anti-platelet antibodies). SLE nephritis represents Type III immune complex disease. Contact dermatitis is Type IV delayed cellular hypersensitivity. Atopic asthma is a classic Type I immediate reaction driven by IgE and Th2 cytokines."],["H","Analyze the diagnostic features of the lupus spectrum by comparing Chronic Discoid Lupus with Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) in terms of: (1) anatomical extent of lesions, and (2) presence of systemic anti-dsDNA antibodies.",["Discoid lupus shows anti-Sm, while SLE shows no autoantibodies","Discoid lupus is skin-limited (face/scalp) with anti-dsDNA rarely present, whereas SLE is multi-organ with anti-dsDNA in 40–60% of patients","Discoid lupus is multi-organ with anti-dsDNA, whereas SLE is skin-limited and anti-dsDNA negative","Both are skin-limited and characteristically anti-dsDNA negative"],1,"Chronic discoid lupus presents with coin-shaped, scaly plaques with follicular plugging limited to the skin. In discoid lupus, severe systemic manifestations and anti-dsDNA antibodies are typically absent, in contrast to the systemic multiorgan involvement characteristic of SLE."],["H","A clinician evaluates three pediatric patients presenting with recurrent bacterial infections:Patient X lacks CD16+ lymphocytes capable of performing ADCC Patient Y has low serum IgM, elevated IgA/IgE, eczema, and thrombocytopeniaPatient Z has normal immunoglobulin levels but phagocytes fail to reduce nitroblue tetrazolium (NBT)Which combination accurately identifies their underlying immunologic disorders?",["X: DiGeorge syndrome; Y: Common variable immunodeficiency; Z: Chediak-Higashi syndrome","X: NK cell deficiency; Y: Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome; Z: Chronic Granulomatous Disease","X: Bruton agammaglobulinemia; Y: Hyper-IgM syndrome; Z: Leukocyte adhesion deficiency","X: Severe combined immunodeficiency; Y: Ataxia-telangiectasia; Z: Myeloperoxidase deficiency"],1,"CD16 is the FcγRIII on natural killer cells. Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome characteristically features thrombocytopenia, eczema, and low IgM with elevated IgA/IgE. The inability to reduce NBT or oxidate dihydrorhodamine confirms deficient respiratory burst due to phagocyte NADPH oxidase failure in Chronic Granulomatous Disease."],["H","An AIDS patient with a CD4+ count of 28 cells/µL presents with respiratory failure and abdominal cramps. Staining of sputum reveals branched fungi, while a colon biopsy demonstrates cytomegalic cells with prominent owl's eye intranuclear inclusions. Analyzing the natural history of untreated HIV, what is the single most common cause of death in this population?",["Kaposi sarcoma","B-cell lymphoma","HIV-associated nephropathy","Opportunistic infections resulting from severe CD4+ T-cell immunodeficiency"],3,"Untreated AIDS culminates in profound loss of cellular immunity, allowing low-virulence environmental pathogens or reactivated latent microbes to establish fatal, disseminated opportunistic infections. These opportunistic infections represent the leading cause of mortality in AIDS patients."],["H","Under what specific molecular circumstances will a circulating Natural Killer cell spare an encountered cell from cytotoxic granule exocytosis?",["When activating receptor signals strictly outweigh inhibitory receptor signals", "When the encountered cell completely downregulates all class I HLA surface molecules", "When the encountered cell expresses massive amounts of surface-bound IgG antibodies", "When inhibitory receptors bind self-MHC class I molecules and dominant inhibitory signaling overrides activating inputs"],3,"NK cell activation is governed by a balance between activating and inhibitory receptors. When healthy cells display self-MHC class I molecules, they engage inhibitory receptors (such as KIRs). This inhibitory signal overrides activating inputs, sparing normal host tissue from lysis."],["H","True or False: In rheumatoid arthritis, anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP) antibodies have a significantly higher diagnostic specificity than rheumatoid factor (RF) because RF is simply an IgM antibody directed against the Fc tail of IgG and can be found in other systemic conditions.",["TRUE","FALSE"],0,"Rheumatoid factor is an autoantibody (usually IgM) that binds the Fc portion of autologous IgG. Because RF can occur in other autoimmune diseases, chronic infections, and elderly individuals, it is less specific than anti-CCP, which targets neoepitopes produced by citrullination and possesses &gt;95% specificity for RA."],["H","Analyze why public health guidelines establish three distinct cutoff thresholds (5 mm, 10 mm, 15 mm) for interpreting the tuberculin skin test (PPD).",["Because induration size varies only with age and sex", "To standardize reporting across all hospitals regardless of individual risk", "To balance diagnostic sensitivity and specificity according to the individual's pretest probability and immune competence", "To reliably distinguish active tuberculosis from latent infection"],2,"The test cutoff is adjusted to the host's cellular immune capacity. Immunosuppressed patients cannot generate large delayed-type hypersensitivity reactions, requiring a low cutoff (5 mm) to avoid false negatives. Conversely, in low-risk individuals, a 15 mm threshold avoids false positives resulting from environmental mycobacteria or prior BCG immunization."],["H","A 30-year-old pregnant woman with SLE delivers a neonate diagnosed with complete congenital heart block. Cross-placental transfer of which maternal autoantibody is responsible for this fetal conduction defect?",["Anti-dsDNA","Anti-Sm","Anti-Ro/SS-A","Anti-Scl-70"],2,"Maternal IgG antibodies against Ro/SS-A (and La/SS-B) can cross the placenta into fetal circulation. These autoantibodies bind to fetal cardiac conduction tissue and cardiomyocytes, inciting inflammation and fibrosis of the atrioventricular node, resulting in congenital heart block."],["H","Genetic analysis of a male toddler with recurrent staphylococcal hepatic abscesses identifies a complete loss-of-function mutation in the gp91phox gene. His mother carries one wild-type and one mutant allele but is entirely asymptomatic. Explain the genetic inheritance pattern and why the mother is protected.",["Autosomal dominant; the mother has incomplete penetrance of the mutation", "Mitochondrial; the mother transmits the defect only to her daughters", "Autosomal recessive; the mother is protected by a second wild-type allele in every cell", "X-linked recessive; the mother's random X-inactivation (lyonization) leaves ~50% of phagocytes with normal gp91phox, which is sufficient for host defense"],3,"gp91phox is located on the X chromosome (X-linked CGD). Heterozygous females possess a mixed population of normal and oxidase-deficient neutrophils due to random lyonization; having even 10–20% functional neutrophils is generally sufficient to protect against bacterial and fungal infections."],["H","If a patient inherits a complete homozygous loss-of-function mutation in complement component C4, which arm(s) of the complement system remain functionally operational?",["Only the Classical pathway","Both Classical and Alternative pathways","Only the Alternative pathway","Both Lectin and Alternative pathways"],2,"Both the classical and lectin pathways depend on the C4b2a complex (C3 convertase) to activate C3. Deficiency of C4 disrupts both the classical and lectin pathways, leaving only the alternative pathway operational (which utilizes Factor B and Factor D to form C3bBb)."],["H","Distinguish the histopathologic lesions of Class III (focal) versus Class IV (diffuse) lupus nephritis in terms of glomerular percentage involvement.",["Class III (focal) affects less than 50% of glomeruli, whereas Class IV (diffuse) affects 50% or more","Class III (focal) affects 50% or more of glomeruli, whereas Class IV (diffuse) affects less than 50%","Both classes affect exactly 50% of glomeruli","Class III affects only the mesangium, whereas Class IV spares the glomeruli"],0,"By definition, Class III focal proliferative lupus nephritis involves &lt;50% of glomeruli, typically with segmental lesions. Class IV diffuse proliferative nephritis is the most severe and frequent variant, involving ≥50% of glomeruli, and presents with global endocapillary proliferation, wire loops, and crescent formation."],["H","True or False: In classic cell-mediated Type IV hypersensitivity, IFN-γ induces \"alternative\" (M2) macrophage activation, leading primarily to tissue repair and collagen deposition rather than microbial killing.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"Correction: IFN-γ (secreted by Th1 and NK cells) combined with CD40-ligand stimulation drives \"classical\" (M1) macrophage activation. Classical activation upregulates inducible nitric oxide synthase (iNOS), ROS, and lysosomal enzymes for microbicidal killing. \"Alternative\" (M2) activation is conversely induced by Th2 cytokines IL-4 and IL-13 to promote tissue repair and fibrosis."],["H","An individual of European descent exhibits multiple high-risk sexual exposures to HIV-1 but repeatedly tests negative for viral infection and proviral DNA. Subsequent genetic analysis demonstrates a homozygous 32-base-pair deletion (CCR5-Δ32). Explain why this deletion confers resistance to infection.",["Homozygous CCR5-Δ32 prevents normal cell-surface expression of CCR5, so R5 (M-tropic) strains cannot bind and complete gp41-mediated membrane fusion", "The deletion prevents reverse transcription inside the nucleus", "The deletion causes rapid antibody-mediated clearance of HIV", "The deletion increases CXCR4 expression, which blocks viral entry"],0,"Approximately 1% of individuals of European descent carry homozygous CCR5-Δ32 mutations. Without functional CCR5 on their macrophages and T cells, gp120 cannot undergo the secondary conformational change required to trigger gp41 insertion and viral envelope fusion, conferring substantial protection against primary infection."],["H","Both Goodpasture syndrome and SLE involve renal glomerular damage. Which comparative diagnostic feature distinguishes the glomerular pathology of Goodpasture syndrome from that of SLE?",["Goodpasture is non-inflammatory, whereas SLE is an acute suppurative pyogenic reaction", "Goodpasture affects only mesangial cells, whereas SLE exclusively destroys collecting duct epithelium", "Goodpasture is driven by IgE degranulation, whereas SLE involves CD8+ CTL lysis", "Goodpasture displays a smooth, linear immunofluorescence pattern of IgG along the GBM, whereas SLE displays an irregular, granular \"lumpy-bumpy\" immune-complex pattern"],3,"Goodpasture syndrome is a Type II hypersensitivity disease where anti-GBM antibodies bind evenly across the collagenous architecture, yielding continuous linear immunofluorescence. SLE is a Type III hypersensitivity disease where circulating immune complexes deposit irregularly along subendothelial and mesangial zones, yielding granular, \"full-house\" immunofluorescence.--- Evaluating (76-90) ---"],["H","A 29-year-old female presents with recurrent oral ulcers, malar rash, a positive ANA (1:640), and persistent 24-hour proteinuria of 0.8 g. According to the revised classification criteria for SLE, does this patient meet definitive diagnostic criteria?",["No, because anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith antibodies are not yet confirmed","No, because she has only three criteria and needs at least four criteria including a positive Coombs test","Yes, because she meets four criteria (malar rash, oral ulcers, proteinuria >0.5 g/day, positive ANA) including both clinical and immunologic parameters","Yes, because persistent proteinuria in a young female is independently sufficient for SLE"],2,"Classification requires at least four criteria (clinical and immunologic), with at least one clinical and one immunologic criterion. The patient has three clinical criteria (acute cutaneous malar rash, oral ulcers, and renal disorder with proteinuria &gt;0.5 g/24 hr) and one immunologic criterion (abnormal ANA titer), satisfying the diagnostic classification."],["H","A 50-year-old female with known diffuse systemic sclerosis presents with progressive exertional dyspnea and persistent dry cough. High-resolution chest CT shows extensive lower-lobe subpleural basilar reticular opacities and honeycombing. A junior resident argues that her symptoms represent CREST-associated pulmonary arterial hypertension. Critique this assessment based on pathophysiologic differences.",["The assessment is correct; basilar honeycombing is diagnostic of CREST-associated pulmonary arterial hypertension", "The assessment is incorrect; the CT findings indicate interstitial pulmonary fibrosis typical of diffuse systemic sclerosis, whereas PAH without interstitial fibrosis typifies limited scleroderma", "The assessment is incorrect; the findings indicate acute bacterial pneumonia", "The assessment is correct; CREST characteristically produces lower-lobe basilar fibrosis"],1,"Diffuse systemic sclerosis leads to extensive interstitial and perivascular fibrosis of the pulmonary parenchyma (interstitial lung disease / basilar fibrosis). While limited scleroderma (CREST) can produce late pulmonary hypertension, it typically occurs due to isolated pulmonary vascular obliteration in the absence of severe interstitial fibrosis."],["H","True or False: In pemphigus vulgaris, autoantibodies target desmogleins in the intercellular junctions of epidermal cells, causing intraepidermal bullae formation through antibody-mediated activation of proteases and disruption of intercellular adhesions.",["TRUE","FALSE"],0,"Pemphigus vulgaris is a Type II antibody-mediated disease. Antibodies target desmoglein 1 and desmoglein 3, disrupting desmosomal junctions (acantholysis) and releasing epidermal proteases, yielding flaccid, easily ruptured intraepidermal blisters."],["H","When evaluating a patient suspected of having primary Sjögren syndrome, what is the clinical significance of finding positive anti-SS-A (Ro) and anti-SS-B (La) antibodies compared to generic ANA?",["Generic ANA confirms the diagnosis, whereas SS-A and SS-B rule out rheumatoid arthritis","SS-A and SS-B provide diagnostic specificity for ribonucleoprotein autoimmunity and correlate with earlier onset, longer disease duration, and extraglandular manifestations","SS-A and SS-B are false-positive artifacts caused by viral cross-reactivity","SS-A and SS-B antibodies signify the patient has developed acute hemolytic anemia"],1,"Antinuclear antibodies (ANA) are positive in over 95% of patients with systemic autoimmune disorders, making them sensitive but poorly specific. SS-A and SS-B antibodies target ribonucleoproteins and are specific serologic markers for Sjögren syndrome that correlate with systemic/extraglandular disease and neonatal lupus."],["H","Evaluate the pathophysiology of CD4+ T-cell loss in HIV infection. Aside from direct cytopathic lysis by budding virions, identify two major indirect mechanisms contributing to CD4+ T-lymphocyte depletion.",["Activation-induced apoptosis of uninfected bystander cells, pyroptosis from abortive viral replication, and killing of infected cells by HIV-specific CD8+ CTLs","Direct cytopathic lysis by budding virions is the sole mechanism","Complement-mediated lysis of circulating CD4+ cells only","Neutralizing antibody-mediated clearance of infected cells alone"],0,"While productive viral infection and budding physically lyse cells, the vast majority of CD4+ T-cell loss occurs via indirect pathways: continuous aberrant immune activation triggering apoptosis/pyroptosis in bystander cells, and clearance of viral-antigen-expressing T cells by CD8+ CTLs."],["H","A 6-month-old male is admitted for his third episode of Staphylococcus aureus cervical lymphadenitis. An exam shows persistent suppurative granulomatous lymphadenitis. A medical student proposes measuring serum complement C3 and C4 levels as the next diagnostic step. Evaluate this proposed diagnostic strategy:",["It is appropriate because C3 deficiency is the primary cause of recurrent staphylococcal abscesses","It is inappropriate; the clinical picture points to a neutrophil respiratory burst defect (CGD), requiring a dihydrorhodamine or NBT reduction assay instead","It is appropriate because C4 deficiency selectively impairs opsonization of catalase-positive staphylococci","It is inappropriate; an emergency bone marrow aspirate is the mandatory first-line test"],1,"Recurrent deep tissue abscesses and granulomas caused by catalase-positive organisms like S. aureus in early infancy are classic for Chronic Granulomatous Disease (phagocyte oxidase defect). The definitive diagnostic assessment is a neutrophil functional assay (e.g., dihydrorhodamine flow cytometry or nitroblue tetrazolium reduction), not complement levels."],["H","A 28-year-old nurse working in a mycobacteriology reference laboratory receives a routine annual PPD skin test. Her measurement shows 11 mm of induration at 48 hours. The occupational health officer argues the test is negative because she is young, healthy, and exhibits no symptoms. Critique this decision based on clinical criteria.",["The decision is correct; 11 mm is negative because she is asymptomatic","The decision is incorrect; PPD is positive only above 20 mm","The decision is incorrect; an induration of ≥10 mm is positive for mycobacteriology laboratory workers","The decision is correct; only ≥15 mm is positive in healthy young adults"],2,"Tuberculin test thresholds are risk-stratified. For healthcare workers, mycobacteriology laboratory personnel, immigrants from high-prevalence countries, or residents/employees of high-risk congregate settings, the cutoff for a positive reaction is ≥10 mm."],["H","True or False: In rheumatoid arthritis, the presence of circulating rheumatoid factor is entirely sufficient to make a definitive diagnosis, even in an asymptomatic individual without joint pain.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"Correction: A positive RF test alone is never diagnostic; RF is present in approximately 5% of healthy individuals and in numerous other autoimmune and infectious conditions. Diagnosis requires clinical synovitis involving peripheral joints, alongside elevated acute-phase reactants, anti-CCP antibodies, or characteristic radiographic erosions."],["H","Which patient profile exhibits the greatest clinical risk for developing life-threatening pyogenic sepsis and rapidly progressive immune-complex glomerulonephritis?",["A patient with inherited deficiency of C1q", "A patient with inherited deficiency of C2", "A patient with inherited deficiency of C9", "A patient with inherited deficiency of C3"],3,"C3 is the central convergence molecule for the classical, lectin, and alternative pathways. A deficiency in C3 completely eliminates downstream C3b-mediated opsonization and C5a leukocyte chemotaxis, producing severe, recurrent pyogenic infections and a high incidence of immune-complex glomerulonephritis."],["H","In evaluating a renal biopsy showing wire loop lesions in a lupus patient, what pathogenic mechanism justifies the classification of diffuse lupus nephritis (Class IV) as more aggressive and severe than focal lupus nephritis (Class III)?",["Class IV involves <50% of glomeruli with only segmental mesangial lesions","Class IV shows isolated mesangial deposits without any proliferation","Class IV shows global sclerosis with no active inflammation","Class IV involves ≥50% of glomeruli with widespread subendothelial immune deposits, endocapillary proliferation, and frequent crescents"],3,"The widespread involvement (≥50% of glomeruli) in Class IV generates massive glomerular endothelial damage, basement membrane duplication, and crescent formation in Bowman's space. This extensive burden rapidly leads to acute renal insufficiency, hypertension, and nephrotic-range proteinuria."],["H","A 58-year-old female taking hydralazine presents with mild arthralgias and ANA positivity (1:320). Her serum is positive for anti-histone antibodies but negative for anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm. Her BUN and creatinine are normal, and urinalysis is unremarkable. Justify why aggressive immunosuppressive chemotherapy with cyclophosphamide is contraindicated in this patient.",["Anti-histone antibodies indicate a severe SLE flare needing aggressive therapy","Cyclophosphamide is contraindicated solely because of the patient's age","The patient has drug-induced lupus, which is benign, rarely involves kidneys or CNS, and resolves after discontinuation of hydralazine","The patient has Class IV lupus nephritis requiring cyclophosphamide"],2,"Drug-induced lupus features anti-histone antibodies and lacks severe visceral, renal, or central nervous system pathology. Treatment requires withdrawing the culprit medication rather than initiating cytotoxic chemotherapy."],["H","When evaluating an antibody-mediated (Type II) autoimmune disease, which cellular mechanism accounts for tissue destruction when autoantibodies bind to basement membranes rather than circulating single cells?",["Direct phagocytosis of the intact organ by alveolar macrophages", "Clonal expansion of cytotoxic T lymphocytes in the spleen", "Immediate mast cell cross-linking causing systemic anaphylaxis", "Complement fixation yielding C5a chemotaxis and Fc-receptor-mediated neutrophil degranulation with release of lysosomal enzymes"],3,"When autoantibodies bind fixed tissue antigens (e.g., in Goodpasture syndrome or ANCA-associated vasculitis), macrophages cannot phagocytose the entire basement membrane. Instead, complement fixation produces chemoattractants (C5a) and leukocytes engage the antibodies via Fc receptors, releasing reactive oxygen species and proteases that cause localized tissue necrosis."],["H","True or False: Systemic sclerosis is predominantly diagnosed in young men in their late teens to early twenties, with a male-to-female ratio exceeding 3:1.",["TRUE","FALSE"],1,"Correction: Systemic sclerosis demonstrates a marked female predominance, with a female-to-male ratio of approximately 3:1, and characteristically peaks in the fifth to sixth decades of life."],["H","A 36-year-old HIV-positive male presents with severe headache, focal seizures, and multiple ring-enhancing brain lesions on contrast CT. Evaluate the potential etiologies from the provided pathogen list and identify the most likely protozoan pathogen.",["Toxoplasma gondii","Pneumocystis jirovecii","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Cryptococcus neoformans"],0,"Toxoplasma gondii is the protozoan that causes central nervous system opportunistic infections and ring-enhancing brain lesions in patients with advanced AIDS. Other listed protozoans like Pneumocystis cause pneumonia, and Cryptosporidium produces watery diarrhea."],["H","During the late-phase response of a Type I hypersensitivity reaction (occurring 2 to 24 hours after allergen exposure), which recruited leukocyte is primarily responsible for sustained epithelial damage and mucosal remodeling?",["Eosinophils", "CD8+ cytotoxic T cells", "Basophils", "Platelets"],0,"While mast cells drive the immediate phase via histamine and leukotrienes, Th2 cells recruit eosinophils via IL-5 during the late phase. Activated eosinophils release major basic protein and eosinophil cationic protein, sustaining epithelial injury, bronchospasm, and tissue remodeling. --- Creating (91-100) ---"],["H","Order the 6 events of the HIV life cycle chronologically: 1. Membrane fusion via gp41  2. Integration of proviral cDNA by integrase  3. Reverse transcription of viral RNA into cDNA  4. gp120 binding to CD4 and CCR5/CXCR4  5. NF-κB-driven transcription and translation of core proteins  6. Budding of mature virions",["4 → 1 → 2 → 3 → 5 → 6","4 → 1 → 3 → 2 → 5 → 6","1 → 4 → 3 → 2 → 5 → 6","4 → 3 → 1 → 2 → 5 → 6"],1,"The HIV cycle begins with gp120 binding to CD4 and coreceptors, inducing a conformational change that triggers gp41 membrane fusion and capsid entry. Reverse transcriptase generates cDNA, which integrase inserts into the host genome. Cellular activation recruits NF-κB to transcribe viral mRNA, leading to polyprotein translation, viral assembly, and final envelope budding."],["H","Design a step-by-step diagnostic evaluation algorithm for a patient with established SLE who presents with new-onset lower-extremity edema and elevated blood pressure. Specify: (1) Initial screening urinalysis findings, (2) Quantitation threshold for proteinuria, and (3) The definitive gold-standard procedure required to guide immunosuppressive therapy.",["(1) Urinalysis showing hematuria, proteinuria, and RBC casts; (2) persistent proteinuria >0.5 g/24 h; (3) renal core biopsy with LM, IF, and EM to classify lupus nephritis","(1) Urinalysis showing sterile pyuria only; (2) proteinuria >3.5 g/24 h; (3) serum anti-dsDNA titer alone","(1) Urinalysis showing glycosuria; (2) fasting glucose; (3) abdominal CT angiography","(1) Urinalysis showing bilirubinuria; (2) 24-h creatinine clearance; (3) renal ultrasound only"],0,"Lupus nephritis is initially screened with a urinalysis to identify active sediment (erythrocyte casts and proteinuria). Persistent proteinuria exceeding 0.5 g/day fulfills lupus renal criteria. Because treatment differs substantially between classes (e.g., mild Class II mesangial vs. aggressive Class IV diffuse proliferative), a renal biopsy is essential to guide therapy."],["H","You are formulating a comprehensive clinical monitoring protocol for a patient newly diagnosed with primary Sjögren syndrome. Which monitoring plan addresses the exocrine pathology and the most serious neoplastic complication?",["Annual serial audiograms and brain MRI to detect acoustic neuromas","Symptomatic dry-eye/dry-mouth therapy and frequent clinical palpation with ultrasound of salivary glands to monitor for extranodal marginal zone B-cell lymphoma","Monthly renal biopsies to rule out early Goodpasture anti-GBM deposition","Daily skin patch testing with urushiol to assess cellular anergy"],1,"Primary Sjögren syndrome management focuses on alleviating keratoconjunctivitis sicca and xerostomia. Because persistent polyclonal B-cell stimulation confers a marked lifetime risk of extranodal marginal zone B-cell lymphoma (MALToma), surveillance of persistent or asymmetric parotid enlargement is essential."],["H","Which three-step schema describes how circulating immune complexes produce necrotizing vasculitis in Type III hypersensitivity?",["Antibody binding to fixed tissue antigens, complement fixation, then receptor stimulation","CTL-mediated direct cytotoxicity, cytokine release, then delayed cellular infiltration","Formation of circulating antigen-antibody complexes, deposition in vessel walls, then complement activation with neutrophil recruitment and release of proteases/ROS","Direct IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation, histamine release, then vasodilation"],2,"Type III hypersensitivity requires antigen-antibody complex formation in slight antigen excess, followed by deposition in vascular basement membranes. In situ complement fixation cleaves C3a and C5a, which recruit and activate neutrophils via their Fcγ receptors, driving fibrinoid necrosis of the vessel wall."],["H","Which combination correctly contrasts Chronic Granulomatous Disease with Terminal Complement (C5–C9) Deficiency in a child with recurrent infections?",["Both present with catalase-positive infections and are screened by DHR", "CGD: Neisseria species screened by CH50; Complement: catalase-positive bacteria screened by NBT", "CGD: catalase-positive bacteria/fungi screened by DHR or NBT; Complement: Neisseria species screened by CH50", "Both present with Neisseria infections and are screened by CH50"],2,"Phagocyte oxidase defects in CGD render patients vulnerable to catalase-positive microbes that eliminate their own hydrogen peroxide, which is confirmed via respiratory burst testing (DHR/NBT). In contrast, terminal complement defects impair membrane attack complex formation, predisposing to thin-walled Neisseria infections; these are screened by a zero or severely depressed CH50 with normal individual pathway components."],["H","When designing a sequential laboratory testing algorithm for a patient presenting with polyarthritis, fatigue, and facial rash, what is the most cost-effective and diagnostically sound sequence?",["Order anti-Sm and anti-Scl-70 first; if negative, order generic ANA","Screen initially with a sensitive antinuclear antibody (ANA) immunofluorescence assay; if positive, reflex to specific autoantibodies (anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm, anti-Ro/SSA, anti-La/SSB)","Perform an immediate renal biopsy before checking any serum autoantibody titers","Order complement C9 levels followed by a tuberculin skin test"],1,"ANA by immunofluorescence is sensitive (&gt;95%) for systemic autoimmune diseases like SLE, making it the appropriate initial screening test. Once ANA positivity is confirmed, reflex testing for disease-specific autoantibodies (anti-dsDNA, anti-Smith for SLE; anti-Ro/La for Sjögren; anti-Scl-70 for systemic sclerosis) determines the specific diagnosis."],["H","Which set best explains the mechanism, diagnostic marker, and treatment failure in hereditary angioedema?",["IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation releases histamine; serum tryptase is elevated and epinephrine is curative","C3 deficiency causes circulating immune complexes with low CH50; steroids are curative","Factor H deficiency causes uncontrolled alternative pathway lysis; fresh frozen plasma is contraindicated","C1-INH deficiency causes unregulated kallikrein-kinin activation with low C4; edema is bradykinin-driven, so antihistamines and epinephrine fail"],3,"Hereditary angioedema is a bradykinin-mediated disorder caused by C1-INH deficiency, which allows unregulated kallikrein activity. Antihistamines, corticosteroids, and epinephrine target mast-cell-mediated IgE pathways (Type I hypersensitivity) and cannot reverse bradykinin-driven vasodilation and increased vascular permeability."],["H","Which sequence describes how CD4+ T cells, synovial macrophages, and B cells collaborate to destroy joints in rheumatoid arthritis?",["Eosinophils degranulate MBP, Th2 cells secrete IL-5, and fibroblasts undergo metaplasia","Immune complexes deposit in dermal vessels, activate complement, and cause leukocytoclastic vasculitis","Autoreactive T/B cells form RF and anti-CCP immune complexes, macrophages release TNF-α/IL-1, and cytokines drive fibroblast/osteoclast pannus eroding cartilage and bone","Neutrophils release elastase, complement lyses synoviocytes, and mast cells deposit collagen"],2,"Rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis involves both adaptive and innate immunity. Autoreactive T and B cells initiate chronic synovial inflammation, while macrophage-derived TNF-α and IL-1 drive synovial hyperplasia (pannus) and activate osteoclasts, leading to irreversible joint destruction."],["H","You are designing an opportunistic infection prophylaxis regimen for an HIV-positive patient whose CD4+ count drops below 200 cells/µL, and then below 50 cells/µL. Based on risk, which antimicrobial combination addresses the primary pathogens encountered at these thresholds?",["Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole at <200 cells/µL (for Pneumocystis jirovecii), and azithromycin plus visual/retinal surveillance at <50 cells/µL (for Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare and Cytomegalovirus)","Penicillin at <200 cells/µL, and acyclovir alone at <50 cells/µL","Vancomycin at <200 cells/µL, and amphotericin B daily at <50 cells/µL","Ciprofloxacin at <200 cells/µL, and chloroquine at <50 cells/µL"],0,"In HIV disease, CD4+ &lt; 200 cells/µL predisposes to Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, managed prophylactically with TMP-SMX. CD4+ &lt; 50 cells/µL carries high risk for disseminated Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare (MAC) and Cytomegalovirus (CMV) retinitis and colitis, requiring systemic macrolide prophylaxis and vigilant retinal surveillance."],["H","A 44-year-old female presents with Raynaud phenomenon, sclerodactyly, severe xerostomia, bilateral parotid swelling, and symmetric morning stiffness in her wrists and MCP joints lasting 2 hours. Urinalysis shows trace protein with no active sediment. Construct a diagnostic workup containing three autoantibody panels that will clarify whether her syndrome represents Systemic Sclerosis, Sjögren Syndrome, or Rheumatoid Arthritis overlap.",["Anti-centromere and anti-Scl-70 (SSc); anti-SS-A/SS-B (Sjögren); anti-CCP and rheumatoid factor (RA)","ANA only; anti-dsDNA; ANCA","Anti-histone; anti-Sm; cryoglobulins","Anti-Jo-1; anti-Mi-2; anti-SRP"],0,"Connective tissue diseases frequently present with overlapping clinical features. Serologic profiling differentiates systemic sclerosis (anti-centromere / Scl-70), Sjögren syndrome (anti-Ro / La), and rheumatoid arthritis (anti-CCP / RF), allowing for appropriate diagnostic subtyping and targeted organ surveillance."]];;
